[
  {
    "id": "ANCHOR-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Kitchen",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "C-001",
      "C-002",
      "C-004",
      "R-006"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-001",
      "FRAG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-001",
      "B11-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "flour dust in sunlight, warm air, yeast",
    "emotional_signature": "seen without having to ask",
    "body": "Saturday morning. The stepfather is away. The house is quiet the way it is only quiet when he is gone — the air itself relaxes. The grandmother is in the kitchen. She is making bread. The flour rises when she kneads and catches the light from the window above the sink. The child is at the table. She is doing nothing. She does not have to perform. She does not have to monitor. She sits and the grandmother works and neither of them needs the other to do anything. The bread dough makes a sound under the grandmother's hands. It is the sound of something being made, not the sound of something being controlled. The flour dust hangs in the light. The kitchen is warm. The child breathes. The grandmother looks over. Just a look. The look says: I see you. You are real. You do not have to earn this. The child does not smile. She does not need to. She just stays. This kitchen is the measure of every safe room she will ever enter. This quiet is the measure of every real peace she will ever feel. She does not know this yet. She will.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "ANCHOR-002",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Hospital",
    "age": 36,
    "year": "Y36",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "R-002",
      "SG-005",
      "C-004",
      "R-006"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-003",
      "B6-001",
      "FRAG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001",
      "B11-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the weight of a newborn on a chest, the sound that is not crying yet",
    "emotional_signature": "broken open and unable to close",
    "body": "The hospital. He is on her chest. The weight of him. She has carried weapons, equipment, the weight of decisions that killed people. None of that weight is this weight. This weight is ounces. This weight is everything. The seal breaks. Not cracks. Breaks. The person behind the door walks out. The eight-year-old who was not protected sees a baby and the operative sees a vulnerability and the grandmother who is dead sees a kitchen that will be rebuilt, and all of them are in the same body at the same time, looking at the same baby, and none of them know what to do. So they do the only thing they can: they hold him. She holds him. The seal is broken. The operative is still there. The eight-year-old is still there. The grandmother's kitchen is still there. But the door is open and they are all in the same room now and the room has a baby in it and the baby is hers. The first person in her entire life who is hers. She does not say I love you. She holds him. The holding is the saying. The weight is the word. The broken seal is the beginning of everything that comes after.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "ANCHOR-003",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Apartment",
    "age": 37,
    "year": "Y37",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-004",
      "O-001",
      "SG-003",
      "R-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-001",
      "B8-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001",
      "B10-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "cold wood floor against bare feet, laptop glow, baby monitor hum",
    "emotional_signature": "the weight of being two people honestly",
    "body": "The apartment floor. She is sitting on it. The baby is asleep in the next room. The baby monitor hums. The laptop is open. She just finished something. Something remote. Something the director routed to her because she is good at it and because the director understood, before she did, that she could do both. Her hands are on the floor. The floor is cold. These hands did the work. These hands held the baby an hour ago. She is looking at her hands and she is both people at the same time and neither one is pretending. The operative is not on pause. The mother is not on pause. They are both running. Both real. The cold floor is the bridge between them. The floor is solid. The floor is home. The floor is also the ground she stands on when she makes the calls that the director sends her. She does not switch between them. She is both. The integration is not smooth. It is not comfortable. But it is real. She gets up. She checks the baby. She closes the laptop. She makes coffee. She is both people and neither one asks permission from the other.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "ANCHOR-004",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Godfather Conversation",
    "age": 44,
    "year": "Y44",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RD-002",
      "O-005",
      "CT-003",
      "P-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-004",
      "B5-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-003",
      "B11-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "kitchen light, late hour, the director's silence",
    "emotional_signature": "fury that is itself the proof of love",
    "body": "The director is in her kitchen. The protectee is asleep. The director tells her about a situation at the protectee's school. A kid. Bullying. She knows the voice. The voice from the lunchroom. The voice that knows it won't be stopped. She feels the rage bank in her chest. She wants to handle it. She wants to handle it the way she handles things: assess, plan, execute. The director watches her. He does not speak. He lets the silence do what silence does: it waits. She waits. The silence fills. She says: I can fix it. The director says: He's a kid. This is a kid problem. Let it be a kid problem. She is furious. The fury is not about the director. The fury is about the thing the director is right about. She knows he is right. The operational response is wrong for the situation. The situation is a child's problem, and children solve their own problems, and solving it for him is not protection. It is control. It is smothering. She is a professional. She knows the difference between a threat and a problem. This is a problem. She does not act. She lets it be a kid problem. The fury stays in her chest. The fury is the measure of how much she loves. The restraint is the measure of how much she trusts the director. Both are real. Both are in the kitchen at the same time. She does not thank him. She pours him coffee. The coffee is real. The restraint is real. The fury will not leave. It is not supposed to. It is the engine. She just does not let it drive.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "ANCHOR-005",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Finger-Moment",
    "age": 56,
    "year": "Y56",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-004",
      "R-003",
      "P-005",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-003",
      "B10-002",
      "ANCHOR-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "his hand on the doorframe, the sound of the door not closing yet",
    "emotional_signature": "proof that both things worked",
    "body": "He is leaving after dinner. He is an adult. He has a life. He puts his hand on the doorframe. He turns back. He says something. Not important. Not dramatic. A thing about next week. A plan. He is making plans. He is planning his own life. She watches his hand on the doorframe. The hand is not small anymore. The hand that reached for blocks. The hand that wrote DO NOT ENTER. The hand that closed doors she taught him to close. The hand is on the doorframe and he is turning back and he is fine. Not because she protected him. Because she protected him and let him become someone who is fine. The protection and the autonomy are not in conflict anymore. They coexist. The proof is a hand on a doorframe. She stands in the doorway after he leaves. She does not move. The apartment is quiet. The flour is not in the air but the kitchen is warm from dinner and the grandmother is in the room, the way the grandmother is always in the room, in the warmth, in the way she watches, in the way she lets go. She lets go. Not of him. Of the vigilance. Just for a moment. Just for the length of a breath. He is fine. The hand on the doorframe is the proof. The door is not closed yet. He is still there, in the doorway, turning back, and she sees the whole arc at once: the kitchen, the hospital, the apartment, the godfather conversation, the playground, the closed door with the sign, and now this doorframe, this hand, this turning back. The arc completes. She does not cry. She breathes. The flour is in the air sometimes. The grandmother is in the room always. He is fine. She is both people and both people are fine.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-001",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Threat Assessment of a Playground",
    "age": 45,
    "year": "Y45",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "P-002",
      "O-005",
      "CT-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-002",
      "B9-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the geometry of a playground, sight lines mapped without trying",
    "emotional_signature": "the bias is structural and she knows it",
    "body": "She takes him to the playground. She sits on a bench. She watches. The playground is assessed in three seconds: entrances, sight lines, cover positions, the adults present, the children, the ratios. She does not do this on purpose. The assessment runs like a background process. It always runs. The difference now is that she knows it runs with a bias. The bias is: everything is a threat to him. The man on the bench across the playground is probably a grandfather. Her assessment says: unknown male, unaccompanied, position gives sight line to the play structure. The bias says: threat. Her judgment says: grandfather. She holds both. She watches him. She also watches the protectee. The protectee is playing. He is happy. He is not in danger. The bias does not change this. The bias is still running. She sits with the bias and the judgment and the protectee on the play structure and the grandfather on the bench and she does nothing except watch. This is what integrated looks like. The operative runs. The mother watches. Neither one is wrong. The director is the one who decides which one drives when the bias and the judgment disagree.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-002",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Director's Watch",
    "age": 47,
    "year": "Y47",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RD-003",
      "RD-001",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-002",
      "B7-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the director's eyes on the protectee, the quality of the attention",
    "emotional_signature": "love that does not name itself",
    "body": "The director visits. He sits in the kitchen. The protectee is doing homework. The director watches him. The watch is the grandmother's watch. It is present, not performing, not intervening. It sees. The protectee does not notice. The protectee is eleven and thinks the director is just a friend of his mother's. The director is the godfather. The godfather watches the way the grandmother watched: from a distance, with full attention, without naming what the attention is made of. She sees the director watching. She does not comment. She makes coffee. She sets it in front of him. She does not say: you love him. She does not need to. The coffee says it. The watch says it. The silence that is full, not empty, says it. Some things are real without being spoken. The grandmother taught her this. The director knows this. She knows this. The protectee does homework. The director watches. She makes coffee. The kitchen is warm. The flour is in the air sometimes. The grandmother is in the room sometimes. Not as a ghost. As a way of being present. The director inherited the grandmother's watch. She inherited the grandmother's kitchen. The protectee inherited the grandmother's safety. The line runs through all of them.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-003",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Protectee's Boundary",
    "age": 49,
    "year": "Y49",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-004",
      "R-003",
      "P-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a closed door with a sign that says DO NOT ENTER, written in a child's hand",
    "emotional_signature": "he built a door, she taught him how",
    "body": "He is thirteen. He closes his door. Not slams. Closes. He puts up a sign: DO NOT ENTER. She stands in the hallway. The sign is in his handwriting. The door is closed. She taught him to close doors. The locked bathroom. The decision to leave. The seal. She is a person who closes doors. He is a person who closes doors. She taught him this without meaning to. She stands in the hallway and she feels two things at once: pride and fear. Pride because he has a boundary and he is asserting it and boundaries are how you survive. Fear because he has a boundary and she is on the wrong side of it and that is where threats come from. She does not open the door. She knocks. She waits. He does not answer. She knocks again. He says: go away. She goes away. This is the hardest thing she has ever done. Going away when he told her to. But the boundary is real and the boundary is hers, given to him by blood and by example, and she will not break it because breaking it would mean that doors only work when she decides they do, and that is not how doors work. She goes to the kitchen. She makes bread. The flour rises. The door stays closed. He is on the other side of it. She is on this side. Both are real. Both are right.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-004",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Dual Track at Full Operation",
    "age": 52,
    "year": "Y52",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "C-004",
      "R-005",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "ANCHOR-003",
      "B9-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "two laptops on a kitchen table, one encrypted, one not",
    "emotional_signature": "the rhythm of two lives in one day",
    "body": "The morning: she makes breakfast. He eats. She packs a lunch. He leaves for school. The transition happens at the door. She watches him leave the way she watches everything: assessed. The door closes. The morning shifts. She opens the encrypted laptop. The director has sent work. She does the work. The work requires the skills. The skills are sharp. The seal is broken but the skills are intact. They did not leak out when the seal broke. They are still there, still sharp, still professional. She finishes the work. She closes the laptop. She opens the other laptop. She pays bills. She orders groceries. She reads a book review. She picks him up from school. He talks about his day. She listens. The listening is the grandmother's language. The work is the director's language. The bills are the civilian language. Three languages. One person. The rhythm is established. The dual track runs. It has run for sixteen years. It will run until he is grown. The rhythm is: civilian, operational, civilian. The order changes. The presence does not. She is there. For all of it.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-005",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Not Her Fight",
    "age": 48,
    "year": "Y48",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "B-001",
      "B-003",
      "CT-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-001",
      "B7-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a phone call she does not answer, a name that is not his",
    "emotional_signature": "the skills are not a public service",
    "body": "A woman she knows from the building. The woman has a problem. A husband. A situation. The woman has heard things. Rumors about what she used to do. The woman asks: can you help me? She looks at the woman. The woman is not the protectee. The woman's problem is not her problem. The skills are directed. The direction is specific. She says: I am sorry. I cannot help you. She does not explain. She does not apologize beyond the sorry, which is social, not remorse. The woman is hurt. The woman does not understand. She understands. The line is clear. Her capabilities exist for one person. Everyone else gets the civilian version: resources, referrals, the phone number of someone who can help. They do not get her. She is not a public service. She is not a weapon for hire. She is a mother with skills she did not choose and she uses them in one direction only. The woman walks away. She stands in the hallway. She does not follow. She does not reconsider. The line is the line. She drew it at the hospital when she decided who she was going to be. Who she was going to be does not include solving other people's problems with force.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-006",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The First Fever",
    "age": 44,
    "year": "Y44",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "P-002",
      "O-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-017",
      "B9-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-007"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a forehead that is too hot, the thermometer, the specific fear that is not operational",
    "emotional_signature": "the child is the mission and the mission is not controlled and the lack of control is the fear",
    "body": "The fever. The child is hot. The forehead is too warm. The thermometer says 102. The number is data. The data is not good. She assesses. The assessment is: child is febrile, child is responsive, child is breathing normally, child is not in immediate danger. The assessment is correct. The fear is not the assessment. The fear is underneath the assessment. The fear is: the child is the mission and the mission is not controlled. The fever is a variable she cannot map. The fever is a door she cannot lock. The fever is inside the child. She cannot enter the child and secure the room. She can only watch. The watching is the oldest skill. The watching is not enough. She calls the doctor. The doctor says: watch. The doctor says the same thing she already knows. The doctor says: if it reaches 104, go to the hospital. She watches. She watches all night. The fever breaks at four AM. The child sleeps. She does not sleep. The operative does not sleep. The operative kept watch. The watch was the love. The love was the fear. The fear was controlled. The child is cool.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-007",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The School Drop-Off",
    "age": 45,
    "year": "Y45",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "R-005",
      "P-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-006",
      "B3-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-008"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a school entrance, the sound of children, the mapping of a new room",
    "emotional_signature": "letting the child go into a room she has not secured",
    "body": "The school. She drops the child off. The child is five. The child is walking into a building she has not mapped. She has mapped it. She mapped it the day she toured it. The exits are: front door, side door, fire exit north, fire exit south. The teachers are: assessed. The playground is: line of sight from the office window. She has mapped the building. The building is not the house. The building is a school. The school is safe. She lets the child go. The letting go is the hardest thing she has ever done. The letting go is: the child walks into a room she is not in. The room is the world. The world is not safe. She knows this. She lets the child go anyway. The letting go is not trust. The letting go is the grandmother. The grandmother let her reach into the purse. The grandmother let her take the cookie. The grandmother let her be. The letting go is the letting be. The child walks in. The child does not look back. She watches the child not look back. The not-looking-back is the independence. The independence is the goal. The goal hurts.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-008",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Parent-Teacher Conference",
    "age": 46,
    "year": "Y46",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-004",
      "R-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-009"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a small chair, a desk, a teacher who does not know who she is talking to",
    "emotional_signature": "the performance in a room that is not operational, the performance is the civilian self",
    "body": "The parent-teacher conference. The teacher is kind. The teacher says the child is bright. The teacher says the child is social. The teacher says the child is a joy. She sits in the small chair. She is not the operative. She is the mother. The mother is the performance. The performance is not a lie. The performance is the civilian self. The civilian self is the one who bakes bread and plants tomatoes and sits in small chairs and hears that the child is a joy. The operative is in the sealed room. The sealed room is still there. The seal is still there. But the seal is not closed. The seal is open. The person is out. The person is in the small chair. The person is the mother. The mother hears: the child is a joy. The child is a joy. The joy is not the wound. The joy is not the love. The joy is new. The joy is the child. The person stands up. The person shakes the teacher's hand. The person leaves. The operative watches. The operative is always watching. The watching is the love.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-009",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Playground Incident",
    "age": 47,
    "year": "Y47",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "P-003",
      "O-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-007",
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-010"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a playground, a bigger child, the child on the ground, the assessment before the action",
    "body": "The bigger child pushes [PROTECTEE]. [PROTECTEE] falls. She sees it. She is on the bench. She is up before she decides to be up. The assessment runs. The bigger child is seven. The bigger child is not a threat. The bigger child is a child who pushed another child. This is not an operational situation. This is a playground situation. She walks to [PROTECTEE]. She picks [PROTECTEE] up. She does not confront the bigger child. She does not need to. She checks [PROTECTEE]. Scraped knee. Not bleeding heavily. Not a head injury. [PROTECTEE] is crying. The crying is pain and surprise. She holds [PROTECTEE]. She holds [PROTECTEE] the way the grandmother held her hand. The grip says: I am here. You are real. The crying slows. The knee needs a bandage. She has a bandage. She always has a bandage. The operative is prepared. The mother is prepared. The prepared is the love. The love is the grip. The grip holds. [PROTECTEE] stops crying. She puts the bandage on. They go back to the bench.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-010",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Code Word",
    "age": 48,
    "year": "Y48",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "P-001",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-009",
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-011"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a word, spoken once, the weight of teaching a child a signal that could save their life",
    "emotional_signature": "teaching the child the first piece of tradecraft, and the tradecraft is love",
    "body": "The code word. She teaches [PROTECTEE] a word. The word is: basil. The word is the grandmother's herb. The word is the kitchen. The word is also: if someone says they are from me and they do not say basil, you do not go with them. [PROTECTEE] is six. [PROTECTEE] does not understand why. She does not explain why. She says: it is a game. It is not a game. The game is the tradecraft. The tradecraft is the love. The love is: if I am not there, the word is there. The word is the deadbolt. The word is the lock. The word is the night light. She teaches the word once. Once is enough. The child remembers. The child remembers because the word is basil and basil is the grandmother's kitchen and the kitchen is safe and the word means safe. The word is the bridge. The bridge connects the tradecraft to the love. The bridge is the grandmother. The grandmother is the word. The word is basil.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-011",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Dream That Came Back",
    "age": 49,
    "year": "Y49",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-013",
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-012"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[GRANDMOTHER]",
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a kitchen, flour in the air, the grandmother and the child in the same room",
    "emotional_signature": "the dead and the living meet in the kitchen, and the kitchen holds both",
    "body": "The dream returns. The kitchen. The grandmother. The flour. But the child is there now. [PROTECTEE] is in the kitchen. The child is small in the dream. The child is reaching into the grandmother's purse. The grandmother lets the child reach. The grandmother looks at her. The look says: you rebuilt the kitchen. She wakes. The apartment is quiet. [PROTECTEE] is asleep. She goes to the kitchen. The flour is on the counter. She opens the bag. The flour rises. The cloud is the dream. The cloud is the grandmother. The cloud is the child. The cloud is the kitchen. The kitchen holds the living and the dead. The kitchen is the bridge. The bridge is the love. The love is the wound. The wound and the love are the same thing. She stands in the kitchen and the flour is in the air and the dream is fading and the kitchen is not fading. The kitchen is real. The kitchen is hers. The kitchen is the grandmother's. The kitchen is the child's. The kitchen is all three.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-012",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Retirement",
    "age": 50,
    "year": "Y50",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-003",
      "B-004",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-010",
      "B9-010"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[DIRECTOR]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a form, a signature, the same silence as the maternity leave but permanent",
    "emotional_signature": "the work ends, the skills do not, the door closes, the person stays",
    "body": "The retirement. A form. A signature. The door closes. The door does not lock. The skills are in the bones. The bones do not forget. The director signs. The director says: we will be in touch. The in-touch is: the door is closed, not locked. She understands. The retirement is the operative stepping back. The person is already out. The person has been out since the hospital. The retirement is the formal acknowledgment of what already happened. She goes home. The apartment has the nursery, now the child's room. The child is eleven. The child is bright and social and a joy. The child knows the word basil. The child does not know why the word matters. She will know when she needs to know. The retirement is the end of the operative. The end is not the end of the skills. The skills are: watching, mapping, protecting, holding, counting, assessing. The skills are the love. The love is not retired. The love works.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-014",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Conversation About the Stepfather",
    "age": 52,
    "year": "Y52",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "SG-005",
      "B-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-006",
      "B4-012"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the kitchen table, the child's question, the pause before the answer",
    "emotional_signature": "telling the truth about the house without giving the child the house",
    "body": "[PROTECTEE] asks: were you happy as a kid. She pauses. The pause is the assessment. The assessment is: the child is fourteen. The child can handle some truth. The child cannot handle the whole truth. She says: not always. The not always is honest. The not always is not the whole truth. The whole truth is the house and the stepfather and the car and the counting and the walk at midnight. The whole truth is not for fourteen-year-olds. The whole truth is for her. She keeps it. She says: my grandmother made things better. The grandmother is the bridge. The bridge is the truth she can share. The child says: tell me about her. She tells the child about the kitchen. The flour. The purse. The mint. The look. She tells the child about the kitchen. She does not tell the child about the house. The house is hers. The kitchen is theirs. The kitchen is the shared room. The shared room is safe. The shared room is the love.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-015",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The False Alarm",
    "age": 53,
    "year": "Y53",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "O-003",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-006",
      "B7-010"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the phone at 2 AM, the sound that is not the child, the assessment that runs before the brain wakes",
    "body": "The phone rings at two AM. She is awake before the second ring. The assessment runs. The child is in the next room. The child is breathing. She can hear it. The phone is not the child's phone. The phone is hers. The ring is the wrong ring. The wrong ring is: the handler. The handler has not called in three years. She picks up. The voice says: false alarm. The voice hangs up. She puts the phone down. Her heart is at 120 BPM. She breathes. The breath brings it to 80. The assessment is complete. The child is safe. The call was nothing. The nothing was the handler checking the line. The line is still open. She is still on the line. The retirement did not close the door. The retirement closed the door she walks through. The door that opens to her is still there. She stands in the hallway. The child is in the next room. The child is safe. She is 120 BPM becoming 80. The operative is still operative. The operative does not retire. The operative waits.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-016",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Cooking Lesson",
    "age": 54,
    "year": "Y54",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-002",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-006",
      "B8-015"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "flour on the counter, two sets of hands, the specific chaos of teaching someone to knead",
    "emotional_signature": "the kitchen is passed down, the flour is passed down, the love is in the dough",
    "body": "She teaches [PROTECTEE] to bake. The kitchen. The flour. The child's hands are in the flour. The flour is everywhere. The flour is on the counter, on the floor, on the child's face. The child laughs. The laugh is the sound. The sound is not the house. The sound is the kitchen. The sound is the grandmother. The grandmother taught her. She teaches the child. The teaching is the passing down. The passing down is the bridge. The bridge connects the grandmother to her to the child. The bridge is the flour. The flour is in the air. The flour is on the hands. The hands are three sets of hands: the grandmother's, hers, the child's. Only two are visible. The third is in the flour. The third is the ghost. The ghost is the grandmother. The ghost is the kitchen. The ghost is the love. The love is in the dough. The dough rises. The child watches. She watches the child watch. The watching is the love. The rising is the future.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B10-017",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Night the Child Drove",
    "age": 55,
    "year": "Y55",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "C-004",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-008",
      "B10-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the passenger seat, the steering wheel in the child's hands, the road that is not hers to control",
    "emotional_signature": "the child drives and she is in the passenger seat and the passenger seat is the hardest seat",
    "body": "The child drives. She is in the passenger seat. The passenger seat is the hardest seat she has ever sat in. The steering wheel is in the child's hands. The child is sixteen. The child has a license. The license is a door. The door is open. The child drives. She does not grab the wheel. She does not press the brake. She sits. She breathes. The breath is the grandmother. The breath is: let them reach into the purse. Let them take the cookie. Let them be. The letting be is the hardest thing. The letting be is the love. The love is the passenger seat. The passenger seat is the position of trust. The trust is: the child can drive. The child can drive. The car moves. The child stops at the light. The child goes at the green. The child drives. She sits. She watches. The watching is the love. The watching is the oldest skill. The watching is the grandmother's look. The look says: I see you. You are real. You are driving. The driving is the independence. The independence is the goal. The goal hurts. The goal is the kitchen. The kitchen is the letting be.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-001",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Kitchen Echo",
    "age": 57,
    "year": "Y57",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-002",
      "C-001",
      "R-006"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "ANCHOR-001",
      "B9-001",
      "FRAG-001",
      "B6-001",
      "B9-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "flour dust, sunlight, the same window angle forty-seven years later",
    "emotional_signature": "the echo is the original, heard from further away",
    "body": "She makes bread. The flour rises. The light comes through the window at the same angle it came through the grandmother's window. She does not try to recreate the kitchen. The kitchen recreates itself, every time she makes bread, because the kitchen is not a place. It is a feeling. The feeling of being in a room where you do not have to perform. She makes bread and the apartment becomes the kitchen and the kitchen becomes the grandmother's kitchen and the grandmother is in the room the way the grandmother is always in the room: in the warmth, in the flour, in the glance that says I see you. She is the grandmother now. She is the one who makes the room safe. She is the one who watches from the chair. She is the one whose hand is brief on the shoulder, weight and warmth, there and gone. She is both: the child who was seen and the adult who sees. The kitchen holds both. The bread is real. The flour is real. The echo is not nostalgia. It is the original, heard from further away, still clear, still warm, still the measure of every safe room.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-002",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Director's Call",
    "age": 58,
    "year": "Y58",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-004",
      "B-002",
      "RD-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-004",
      "B8-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the phone ringing in an apartment that is quiet",
    "emotional_signature": "the work is still there, but it does not run the day",
    "body": "The director calls. He has work. She listens. She assesses. She says: send it. She does the work. The work is not what it was. It is remote, targeted, specific. The director sends her the things that require her specific skills and he does not send her the things that do not. She does not ask for more. She does not volunteer. The skills are sharp but they are not hungry. The hammer does not look for nails. The skills are tools. She is the person who uses them. She is not the tools. The distinction matters now in a way it did not matter at twenty-eight. At twenty-eight, she was the operative. At fifty-eight, she is a person who has operative skills. The order of the nouns has changed. The director understands this without being told. The work comes. She does the work. The work ends. She makes dinner. The phone does not ring again until the next time. The next time will come. She will be ready. Readiness is not the same as hunger. She is ready. She is not hungry. The protectee is grown. The apartment is quiet. The flour is in the air sometimes. The grandmother is in the room always. The director calls. She answers. The work is real. The dinner is real. Both.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-003",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "Folding Back",
    "age": 59,
    "year": "Y59",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-002",
      "SG-005",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-003",
      "B8-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the 2 AM apartment, the same hum, the same monitor, empty now",
    "emotional_signature": "the sealed parts breathe, and she lets them, and she does not follow",
    "body": "Two in the morning. The apartment is quiet. The monitor is gone. He does not need it. He is grown. But at two in the morning, the apartment still breathes the way it breathed when the monitor hummed. The sealed parts shift. She does not open them. She does not revisit them. But she feels them with more precision now, not less. The integration means she can feel the sealed parts without being consumed by them. The cost is carried. The cost is known. The things in the room behind the door are still there. They are still heavy. But she is larger now than she was when she sealed them. The room is the same size. She is not. She breathes. The sealed parts breathe with her. She does not follow them into the room. She stands at the door. The door is closed. The lock is hers. She decides when it opens. It does not open. But she stands at the door and she acknowledges what is behind it. This is what mature integration looks like. The sealed parts are not denied. They are not indulged. They are carried. She gets up. She makes tea. The morning will come. The morning is civilian. The sealed parts stay in their room. She stays in the kitchen. The flour is not in the air at two in the morning. But the kitchen is still warm from the day before. The warmth is enough.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-004",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Director Calls It",
    "age": 57,
    "year": "Y57",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "CT-002",
      "RD-002",
      "CT-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "ANCHOR-004",
      "B10-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the director's voice saying no, the phone going quiet",
    "emotional_signature": "the hardest deference is when you could do it yourself",
    "body": "She has a plan. The plan would work. The plan would eliminate a threat to the protectee. The plan is operationally sound. The plan crosses the containment line: the consequences of executing it would expose the protectee to legal jeopardy, social fallout, a life where his mother is not there because she is somewhere else, behind a door that does not open. She calls the director. She presents the plan. She presents it with the precision she brings to everything. The director listens. The director says no. She is furious. The fury is the same fury as the godfather conversation: the fury of wanting to act and being told not to. But the director is right. He is right because her threat model is biased. He is right because the plan solves the operational problem and creates the life problem. He is right because she would cross the line to protect him and the line exists to protect him from her. She defers. She does not execute the plan. She closes the laptop. She makes tea. The fury stays in her chest. The fury is the engine. The deference is the brake. Both are real. Both are necessary. The director called it. She accepts the call. This is what trust means: accepting the call when the call is against you.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-005",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Flour in the Air",
    "age": 60,
    "year": "Y60",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "C-002",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-016",
      "B10-011",
      "B8-015"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the kitchen, flour rising, the same cloud, fifty-five years of the same cloud",
    "emotional_signature": "the kitchen is the constant, the kitchen is the love, the kitchen is the grandmother who is still there",
    "body": "She opens the bag. The flour rises. The cloud is the same cloud that rose in the grandmother's kitchen when she was five. The same cloud that rose in the bakery when she was nineteen. The same cloud that rose in the apartment when she was thirty-four. The same cloud that rose when she taught the child to knead. The flour is the constant. The flour is the grandmother. The flour is the kitchen. The kitchen is the room that does not change. The rooms change. The cities change. The seal opened. The child was born. The work ended. The child drove. The passenger seat was the hardest seat. The flour does not change. The flour rises. She is sixty. The child is twenty-one. The child is in the kitchen. The child is kneading. The child learned from her. She learned from the grandmother. The grandmother is in the flour. The flour is in the air. The air is the kitchen. The kitchen is the love. The love is the wound. The wound and the love are the same thing. They have always been the same thing. The flour rises. The kitchen holds.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-006",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Call From the Child",
    "age": 61,
    "year": "Y61",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "P-005",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-007",
      "B10-014"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the phone, the child's voice, the distance that is not the house",
    "emotional_signature": "the child calls and the call is the reverse of the call she made at nineteen",
    "body": "The child calls. The child is twenty-two. The child is in a different city. The child is happy. The child is building a life. The child says: how are you. She says: fine. The fine is the family word. The fine is the word she said to the counselor. The fine is the word her mother said on the phone. But this fine is different. This fine is true. She is fine. The apartment is quiet. The nursery is a guest room now. The child does not live here. The child visits. The child calls. The call is the reverse. She called her mother at nineteen. Her mother said fine. The fine was a lie. She says fine now. The fine is true. The reversal is the kitchen. The kitchen is the thing she built. The kitchen is the thing the grandmother built. The kitchen is the thing she passed down. The child has the kitchen. The kitchen is in the child. The child calls. She answers. They talk. The talk is not about the house. The talk is about the kitchen. The kitchen is the love. The love is the bridge. The bridge holds.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-007",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Mirror at Sixty-Two",
    "age": 62,
    "year": "Y62",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-002",
      "C-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-007",
      "B8-018"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "her face, older, the lines that are the map of the rooms she has been in",
    "emotional_signature": "the face is the operative and the person and the grandmother, all three",
    "body": "She looks in the mirror. The face is older. The lines are the map. The map is: the house, the kitchen, the apartment, the cities, the hotel rooms, the hospital, the playground, the passenger seat. The lines are the rooms. The rooms are the life. The face is the operative and the person and the grandmother. The operative is in the jaw. The person is in the eyes. The grandmother is in the hands. She holds the hands up. The hands are the grandmother's hands now. The skin is thin. The grip is still strong. The hands kneaded dough. The hands held the child. The hands locked the deadbolt. The hands reached into the purse and took the mint. The hands are the grandmother's hands. She is the grandmother now. She is the kitchen. She is the look that says: I see you. You are real. The mirror shows three people. The three people are one person. The one person is her. She is sixty-two. She is the grandmother. She is the operative. She is the person. She is all three. The mirror shows all three. The mirror does not lie.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-008",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Basil Plant",
    "age": 63,
    "year": "Y63",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-002",
      "P-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B10-010",
      "B10-016",
      "B9-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "basil on the windowsill, the smell of it, the word that is the code and the herb",
    "emotional_signature": "the word is the plant, the plant is the kitchen, the kitchen is the grandmother, the grandmother is the love",
    "body": "She grows basil. On the windowsill. The basil is the word. The word is the code. The code is: basil. The child knows the word. The word means safe. The basil on the windowsill is safe. The basil grows. The basil needs water and light. The water and light are the care. The care is the kitchen. The kitchen is the grandmother. The basil is the grandmother's herb. The herb is the kitchen. The kitchen is the love. The love is the wound. The wound and the love are the same thing. The basil grows. She waters it. She puts it in the light. The basil is the living kitchen. The kitchen was flour and heat and the grandmother looking over. The kitchen is now basil on the windowsill. The basil is the grandmother, still growing, still here, still the kitchen. She touches a leaf. The leaf is soft. The smell is the kitchen. The kitchen is in the leaf. The leaf is the love. The love is alive. The love grows. The basil grows. The kitchen grows.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B11-009",
    "bucket": 11,
    "title": "The Quiet",
    "age": 65,
    "year": "Y65",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-002",
      "C-001",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B11-005",
      "B11-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the apartment, the afternoon light, the sound of nothing that needs attention",
    "emotional_signature": "the quiet is not the house's quiet, the quiet is the kitchen's quiet, and the difference is everything",
    "body": "The quiet. The apartment is quiet. The quiet is not the house's quiet. The house's quiet was the space between the stepfather's moods. The house's quiet was the waiting for the car in the driveway. This quiet is the kitchen's quiet. The kitchen's quiet is the flour settling. The kitchen's quiet is the basil on the windowsill. The kitchen's quiet is the child calling from another city. The kitchen's quiet is the grandmother, still in the flour, still in the hands. She sits in the kitchen. The afternoon light is through the window. The basil is on the sill. The flour is on the counter. The child's room is a guest room. The apartment is hers. The quiet is hers. The quiet is the first quiet she has ever owned. The house's quiet was borrowed. The hotel rooms' quiet was operational. This quiet is the kitchen's quiet. The kitchen's quiet is the love. The love is the wound. The wound is the grandmother. The grandmother is the quiet. The quiet is hers. She sits. She breathes. The quiet holds.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-001",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "Summer and Not-Summer",
    "age": 8,
    "year": "Y8",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-001",
      "C-002",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the difference between two front doors",
    "emotional_signature": "two lives, one body",
    "body": "Summer is the grandmother's house. The door is a screen door. It bangs when you go out. The grass is warm. The air outside is different air. Not-summer is home. The door is heavy. It closes quiet because loud means something. The air inside is thick. She knows which door she is walking toward before she gets there. The summer door she runs to. The not-summer door she checks. Two houses. Two doors. Two kinds of air. One child who walks different depending on which one is in front of her. She does not think about this. She just does it. The body knows which house it is in.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-002",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "The Table Where She Isn't",
    "age": 9,
    "year": "Y9",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "SG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "her_mother",
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of forks on plates when nobody speaks",
    "emotional_signature": "the absence that sits in the chair next to you",
    "body": "Dinner. The stepfather eats. The mother eats. She eats. Nobody speaks. The forks make sounds. The plates make sounds. The refrigerator hums. She watches her mother. Her mother is looking at her plate. She is present in the room and absent from it at the same time. The child wants to say something. She does not. She has learned that silence at this table is not emptiness. It is the space between the stepfather's calm and whatever comes after. She eats. She watches. She does not speak. The absence in the chair next to her is her mother's body, present and useless, and the child does not blame her because the child already knows that being present and being able are not the same thing.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-003",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "The Bully's Voice",
    "age": 8,
    "year": "Y8",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "P-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-001",
      "B8-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the specific pitch of a voice that knows it won't be stopped",
    "emotional_signature": "the sound of someone testing how much they can take",
    "body": "A girl at school. Bigger. Older. The voice is the thing. Not the shove. The shove is small. The voice is the weapon. It says things about her clothes, her hair, the way she walks. The voice is loud enough that other kids hear. The other kids do nothing. Not cruelty. Invisibility. They look away. She stands there. She does not cry. She has already learned that crying in front of someone who wants you to cry is giving them what they came for. The rage is there. It is the first time she feels it as a separate thing, not just fear. It is hot and it wants a target. She holds it. Not because she is calm. Because she knows that if she spends it here, on this girl, she will not have it later, when she might need it more. The first lesson in holding rage: it is a resource, not a reaction.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-004",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "Grandmother's Hands",
    "age": 7,
    "year": "Y7",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-002",
      "R-006"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-002",
      "B11-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the weight and warmth of a hand on a shoulder, brief and specific",
    "emotional_signature": "presence without performance",
    "body": "She is sitting on the couch at the grandmother's house. She is not doing anything. The grandmother walks past. A hand on her shoulder. Brief. Not a hug. Not a squeeze. Just weight and warmth, there and gone. The child does not move. She does not need to. The hand said everything the hand needed to say. She is real. She is seen. She does not have to earn this. Later, when the grandmother is gone, the child will remember the weight of that hand more clearly than any word anyone ever spoke to her. Presence is not performance. The hand was not performing. It was being there.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-006",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "The Report Card",
    "age": 9,
    "year": "Y9",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-005",
      "O-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B5-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather",
      "her_mother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of paper being turned face-down on a table",
    "emotional_signature": "achievement is safe when it is small",
    "body": "The report card. Good grades. Not excellent. Good. She brings it home. She hands it to her mother. Her mother looks at it. Her mother puts it on the table. The stepfather picks it up. He reads it. He puts it down. Face down. The face-down means: not bad enough to mention, not good enough to praise. She has calibrated correctly. Excellent would have drawn attention. Failure would have drawn attention. Good is invisible. Good is safe. She will be good for the rest of her childhood. Not excellent. Not failing. Good. The space where nobody looks. She lives there.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-007",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "The Girl Who Shared Lunch",
    "age": 7,
    "year": "Y7",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-001",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-006"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a sandwich cut in half, the way someone slides it across a table",
    "emotional_signature": "generosity from someone who does not have to",
    "body": "She forgot her lunch. A girl she barely knows hands her half a sandwich. The girl does not explain. The girl does not make a thing of it. The girl just slides the sandwich across the table and goes back to eating. The sandwich is peanut butter. The bread is white. It is the best sandwich she has ever eaten because it was given without condition and without performance and without being asked. She does not thank the girl loudly. She nods. The girl nods back. The exchange is complete. She will remember this girl for the rest of her life, even though she never learns her name. The gesture is the grandmother's gesture: presence without performance, given without asking.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-008",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "The Night the Power Went Out",
    "age": 8,
    "year": "Y8",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-010"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "her_mother",
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "total darkness, the sound of a refrigerator winding down",
    "emotional_signature": "the dark is not the danger, the reaction to the dark is the danger",
    "body": "The power goes out. The house is dark. Total dark. The refrigerator stops humming. The silence is loud. She is in her room. She does not move. She listens. The stepfather is swearing somewhere in the house. Her mother is looking for candles. The stepfather's voice is the danger. Not the dark. The dark is just dark. The voice means he is off-rhythm, he cannot control the dark, and things he cannot control make him worse. She stays in her room. She finds the edge of the bed by touch. She sits. She waits. The candles come on. The stepfather calms. She has survived another thing that was not the thing itself but the reaction to the thing. She is eight. She is already a person who assesses the reaction, not the event.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B2-009",
    "bucket": 2,
    "title": "The Neighbor Who Noticed",
    "age": 9,
    "year": "Y9",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "P-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-007"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a neighbor's face through a fence gap, the quality of attention",
    "emotional_signature": "being seen by someone who is not supposed to see you",
    "body": "She is in the backyard. A neighbor looks through the fence gap. The neighbor is an older woman. The neighbor looks at her and the look is the wrong kind. Not dangerous. Observing. The neighbor sees something. The neighbor sees the child who is too quiet, too watchful, too small for her age. The neighbor does not say anything. The neighbor looks. The child sees the neighbor looking. The child knows what the neighbor sees. The child also knows that if the neighbor says something, it will not help. It will make it worse. The stepfather will know someone is watching. The stepfather will adjust. The adjustment will be worse than the current state. The child does not wave at the neighbor. She holds the neighbor's gaze. Then she looks away. The message is: I see you seeing me. Do not intervene. The neighbor does not intervene. The neighbor closes the curtain. The child goes back to being quiet in the yard.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B3-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Performance",
    "age": 11,
    "year": "Y11",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-005",
      "SG-004",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather",
      "her_mother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the smell of a clean house that was cleaned in fear",
    "emotional_signature": "the performance is survival",
    "body": "The stepfather is bringing someone home. The house has to be right. The mother cleans with a focus that is not about cleanliness. The child is told to be good. She knows what good means. Good means quiet. Good means present but invisible. Good means eating without chewing sounds. Good means laughing at the right times and not at the wrong ones. She practices. She stands in her room and practices being good. The performance is not acting. It is adaptation. The person she becomes when the stepfather needs the house to look right is a real person. She just isn't the same person who runs through the grandmother's grass. Both are real. One is for survival. One is for life. She is eleven and she already knows the difference, even though she does not have the words for it yet.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B3-002",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "Social Math",
    "age": 12,
    "year": "Y12",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B5-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the exact distance between her desk and the popular girls' cluster",
    "emotional_signature": "mapping a room you will never belong to",
    "body": "She sits where she sits. The popular girls sit where they sit. The distance between is measurable. She has measured it. She watches how they move, what they say, who they include and who they cut. She is not trying to join. She is trying to understand. The social world is a system. Systems have rules. She cannot feel the rules the way they feel them, but she can observe the rules the way you observe a patrol pattern. Who moves first. Who follows. Who gets cut and what they did wrong. She is twelve and she is doing threat assessment on a lunchroom. She does not know this is what she is doing. She thinks she is just watching. But the watching has a structure. The structure will save her life later. Right now it just keeps her from making the wrong move in a room where the wrong move means being the target.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B3-003",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "Grandmother Watching",
    "age": 11,
    "year": "Y11",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "RD-003",
      "C-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B5-002",
      "B6-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the stillness of someone watching from a window",
    "emotional_signature": "love that cannot intervene and does not look away",
    "body": "The grandmother comes to the house. Not often. The stepfather tolerates these visits. The child is in the living room. The grandmother sits in a chair. She does not say much. She watches. She watches the child, and she watches the room, and she watches the way the child moves in the room. She sees the performance. She does not break it. She cannot break it. But she looks at the child in a way that says: I see both of you. I see the one who runs and the one who performs. Both are real. Neither is wrong. You are surviving. The child does not hear these words. She feels them. The grandmother's eyes on her are different from any other eyes. They see without judging. They stay without rescuing. The grandmother will die in seven years. The child does not know this. But the way the grandmother watches — the way she is present without intervention — is the template for every relationship that matters after. The director will watch her the same way.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B3-004",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Grandmother's Purse Revisited",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-003",
      "R-006"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-009"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B5-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the same leather purse, the same peppermint, but she is taller now",
    "emotional_signature": "continuity that does not need words",
    "body": "The grandmother visits. The purse is on the table. The same purse. The child does not need to reach in anymore. She is ten. She sits next to the purse and the grandmother pushes the mint tin toward her without looking. The gesture is practiced. Five years of the same gesture. The child takes a mint. The grandmother takes a mint. They sit together and eat mints and nobody talks. The silence is the same silence as the kitchen. Full, not empty. The purse is still open. The child is still welcome. The mints are still sweet and sharp. She is taller now. The grandmother is older now. The gesture has not changed. Some things do not change. She files this. Things that do not change are the safest things. She will look for things that do not change for the rest of her life.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B3-006",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Friend Who Came Over",
    "age": 12,
    "year": "Y12",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-005",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B5-006"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of her own voice pitched higher than normal, the effort of performance",
    "emotional_signature": "performing normal for someone who does not know the house",
    "body": "A friend from school comes over. She has never invited anyone. The stepfather agreed. The house must look right. She cleans her room. She straightens the living room. She tells the friend to take off their shoes. She tells the friend to be quiet. She tells the friend too many things. The friend looks at her. The friend does not understand why there are so many rules. She sees the friend not understanding. She adjusts. She performs casual. She performs normal. The performance is exhausting. The friend leaves. She closes the door and she is alone and she does not have to perform anymore and the relief is physical, like taking off a tight shoe. She will not invite anyone again for two years. The performance costs too much. She learns that having people in the house means performing the house, and the house is too expensive to perform.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B3-008",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Time He Was Nice",
    "age": 12,
    "year": "Y12",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-004",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-007"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of laughter that is not preceded by silence",
    "emotional_signature": "the worst thing is when the danger is kind, because kindness breaks the pattern and the pattern is how you survive",
    "body": "He is nice. One night. No reason. He makes a joke. He laughs. Her mother laughs. The house is warm. She does not know what to do with this. The niceness is worse than the silence. The niceness breaks the pattern. She has calibrated herself to the pattern. The silence, the tension, the release. The niceness is outside the pattern. She does not know where to put her body. She does not know what face to make. She makes the performance face. The face that says: this is normal, I am normal, we are normal. She is twelve and she knows that the niceness is the most dangerous thing he does because it makes the performance feel real and the performance is not real and if she forgets that, she will not survive. She files the niceness under: anomaly. She does not let it change the calibration.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-001",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Locker Room",
    "age": 13,
    "year": "Y13",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "SG-004",
      "P-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-006",
      "B2-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-003",
      "B8-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the smell of industrial cleaner and sweat, fluorescent buzz",
    "emotional_signature": "rage held in a body too small for it",
    "body": "The locker room. Two girls. They have been circling her for weeks. Today they close. One blocks the door. The other talks. The voice is the same voice from the lunchroom three years ago but bigger now, and it has learned to be cruel with precision, not volume. The words are about her body. The way it moves. The way it doesn't. The other girls in the room pretend not to hear. The fluorescent light buzzes. The cleaner smell is sharp. The rage in her chest is a physical thing. It wants out. It wants this girl's face. It wants to spend itself. She feels it in her hands, her jaw, the back of her throat. She does not move. She holds it. Not because she is calm. Because she is not calm, and the not-calm is a resource, and if she spends it here she will not have it later. The girl at the door laughs. She walks past her. The girl lets her. She wins nothing and loses nothing and the rage stays in her chest, banked, waiting. She will learn to use it. Not now. But the holding is the first skill.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-002",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Sound Remembers",
    "age": 14,
    "year": "Y14",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-004",
      "O-003",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-002",
      "B8-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a cabinet closing in a house that is not her house",
    "emotional_signature": "the body knows before the mind agrees",
    "body": "She is at a friend's house. The friend's father closes a cabinet. Not hard. Normal. The sound hits her before she knows why. Her chest tightens. Her hands go still. She is watching the friend's father's back and she is also watching the stepfather's back and she does not know which one is real. The friend's father turns around. He is smiling. He says something about snacks. He is not the stepfather. She knows this. Her body does not know this. Her body has learned the sound of too much force on a cabinet and it does not care whose hand made it. She stays. She breathes. She watches the friend's father until her body agrees that he is not the threat. This takes longer than it should. She knows it takes longer than it should. She does not talk about it. She eats the snack. The cabinet sound stays in her chest like a held breath.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-003",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "Two Lives Now",
    "age": 14,
    "year": "Y14",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-005",
      "SG-005",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-001",
      "B9-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "two different pairs of shoes on two different porches",
    "emotional_signature": "the person you are depends on which door you walked through",
    "body": "Summer. The grandmother's house. She is fourteen now and the duality is not a feeling anymore, it is a structure. At home she is quiet, watchful, small. At the grandmother's she is louder, bigger, real. The transition happens at the door. She can feel herself change as she walks up the porch steps. By the time the screen door bangs she is the other one. The one who speaks. The one who exists without asking permission. The grandmother watches this happen. The grandmother never comments. The grandmother makes lunch. The child eats lunch as the real one, and the real one tastes the food, and the not-real one back home does not taste anything, just performs eating. Two people in one body. She does not know if this is normal. She suspects it is not. She does not ask.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-004",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "Identity Crisis",
    "age": 15,
    "year": "Y15",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-005",
      "C-004",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "her own face in a bathroom mirror, unfamiliar",
    "emotional_signature": "who is the one who looks back",
    "body": "The bathroom mirror. She does not recognize herself. Not in the way adolescents don't recognize themselves — not the body, not the skin. The face. The face looks back at her and she does not know which one it is. The quiet one or the real one. The one who survives or the one who lives. The mirror does not tell her. She stands there for a long time. The face in the mirror is tired. It is the face of someone who has been performing for so long that the performance might be the person. She does not cry. She presses her hands flat against the glass. The glass is cold. The hands are warm. The cold and the warm are both real. She decides that real is what she can feel. Everything else is a story. She does not know if this is wisdom or survival. At fifteen, the difference does not matter.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-006",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Girl From Lunch",
    "age": 14,
    "year": "Y14",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-001",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of a laugh that is not performing, two people eating lunch on a curb",
    "emotional_signature": "friendship that does not require the performance",
    "body": "The girl from lunch. Seven years ago, half a sandwich. They are fourteen now. They eat lunch together every day. Not in the cafeteria. On the curb behind the gym. The curb is theirs. Nobody watches. They talk about nothing. Music. Teachers. A movie. Nothing important. The nothing is the point. The nothing is the space where she does not have to be the quiet one or the watchful one or the one who counts steps. She is just a girl on a curb eating lunch with a friend. The friend does not know about the house. The friend does not know about the stepfather. The friend knows the version of her that sits on curbs and talks about nothing. The version is real. It is not the whole version. It is a real version. She can be real here. The curb is a room without walls. The friend is a safe person. The lunch is unremarkable. The unremarkable is the measure of safety.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-007",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Nice Returns",
    "age": 14,
    "year": "Y14",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-005",
      "SG-004",
      "B-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-008",
      "B2-009"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B5-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather",
      "her_mother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of ice in a glass, a weekend afternoon, the house uncharacteristically calm",
    "emotional_signature": "kindness that is not kindness, it is the absence of the usual, and the absence is the trap",
    "body": "He is nice again. A weekend. Ice in a glass. Music from another room. The mother is relaxed. The stepfather is in a good mood. The house is almost normal. She does not trust it. She is fourteen and she has learned that the nice is a cycle. The nice comes and the nice goes and the going is worse than the not-nice that was there before the nice. She watches her mother. Her mother is smiling. Her mother believes the nice. Her mother always believes the nice. The child does not believe the nice. She waits for the nice to end. The waiting is the skill. The nice will end. She does not know when. She knows it will. She is right. By Monday, the house is the house again. The ice is gone. The music is gone. The mother is the mother again. The child is the child again. The nice was a window that closed. She does not stand in windows. She waits for them to close.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-009",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Art Teacher",
    "age": 15,
    "year": "Y15",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-003",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "charcoal dust, the smell of fixative, a teacher's hand on her shoulder for a moment",
    "emotional_signature": "being seen for what she makes, not for what she performs",
    "body": "The art teacher looks at her drawing. The drawing is not good. It is real. The teacher sees something in it. The teacher says: you see differently. The child does not understand. The teacher says: most people draw what they think things look like. You draw what you see. The child shrugs. The teacher puts the drawing on the wall. The child does not want it on the wall. The wall is visibility. Visibility is danger. But the teacher put it there and the teacher is an authority and she does not challenge authorities she does not need to challenge. She looks at the drawing on the wall. It is hers. It is on the wall. It is visible. She is visible. She survives the visibility. Nothing bad happens. This is a new data point. Visibility is sometimes safe. She files this. The file is small. It will grow.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-010",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Party",
    "age": 15,
    "year": "Y15",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "R-005",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "loud music, the smell of cheap cologne and spilled soda, the geometry of a crowded room",
    "emotional_signature": "reading a room full of people who are not reading her",
    "body": "A party. She does not want to go. She goes. The friend from the curb wants to go. The party is loud. The music is too loud. She maps the room in three seconds: exits, rooms, the kitchen, the back door. She stays near the back door. The friend is on the dance floor. She watches. She reads the room. Who is drinking. Who is watching who. Who is about to do something stupid. She does not know she is doing this. She thinks she is just standing there. She is standing there and running an assessment. The friend comes back. The friend is happy. The friend does not notice the assessment. Good. The assessment is invisible. She wants it to be invisible. She wants to be the person who is at the party, not the person who is watching the party. She is both. She is fifteen and she is already both and she does not know if both is normal. She suspects it is not.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-011",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Night She Left the House",
    "age": 15,
    "year": "Y15",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "B-004",
      "SG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B5-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the front door at midnight, the cold air outside, the street empty",
    "emotional_signature": "leaving before the thing happens is better than leaving after",
    "body": "She leaves the house. Not permanently. She walks out the front door at midnight because the stepfather is in a mood and the mood is escalating and she knows the escalation pattern and she does not want to be in the house when it peaks. She walks. The street is empty. The air is cold. She walks to the end of the block and back. The house is quiet when she returns. The peak passed. She goes to her room. She is not running away. She is running the clock. The mood has a duration. The duration is predictable. She waits it out somewhere else. She does not know this is a tactical withdrawal. She is fifteen. She calls it going for a walk. The walk is a skill. The walk is how you survive a thing without being in the room where the thing happens. She will use this skill again. In different houses. In different countries. For different reasons. The walk is always the same. Leave. Wait. Return.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-012",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The School Counselor",
    "age": 14,
    "year": "Y14",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-005",
      "SG-005",
      "B-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-009"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a small office, the smell of coffee and paper, a question that is too specific",
    "emotional_signature": "the system is not the solution, the system is the risk",
    "body": "The school counselor calls her in. The counselor asks questions. How is home. How is the family. How are things. The questions are too specific. The counselor has heard something. Maybe the neighbor. Maybe a teacher. The child lies. The lies are smooth. The lies are the performance. The performance is perfect. The counselor writes something down. The child sees the writing. The counselor says: you can talk to me. The child knows she cannot. The counselor is the system. The system can intervene. Intervention makes it worse. The stepfather will know. The stepfather will adjust. The adjustment will be worse than the current state. She smiles. She says everything is fine. She leaves. The counselor does not call her back. The counselor believed the performance. The performance worked. The performance is not a lie. The performance is a wall. The wall is load-bearing.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B4-013",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Journal",
    "age": 15,
    "year": "Y15",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-003",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a notebook with a lock, the sound of the click, the pen on paper that is only hers",
    "emotional_signature": "the one place where the performance stops",
    "body": "She keeps a journal. A notebook with a lock. The lock is cheap and would not stop anyone who really wanted in. The lock is a symbol. The symbol says: this is mine. She writes in it at night. She writes about the house. She writes about the stepfather. She writes about the grandmother. She writes about the rage. The rage on paper is different from the rage in her chest. The rage on paper has been spent. The rage in her chest is banked. She writes until the rage on the page is enough for tonight. Then she locks the book. She hides it. She sleeps. The journal is the first safe room she builds herself. Not the grandmother's kitchen. Not the summer grass. A room made of paper and a cheap lock. The room is hers. She built it. She decides what goes in it. She decides who reads it. Nobody reads it. The room stays safe.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B5-001",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "The First Boundary",
    "age": 16,
    "year": "Y16",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "B-003",
      "SG-001",
      "R-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-003",
      "B10-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of her own voice saying no, unfamiliar in her throat",
    "emotional_signature": "the first time the wall pushes back",
    "body": "He tells her to do something. Not large. Small. A thing she has done a hundred times. But today she does not want to. She does not have a reason. She just does not want to. She says no. The word comes out of her throat and it is a sound she has not made before, directed at him. The room goes quiet. He looks at her. She looks at him. The look lasts three seconds. It feels like a year. He turns away. He does not push. She does not know why. Maybe he is tired. Maybe the no surprised him. Maybe he calculated that pushing was not worth it today. She does not know and she does not care. The no happened. She said it. The word was real. She walks to her room and closes the door and sits on the bed and her hands are shaking because she did not know she could do that. She did not know the no was in her. It was. It is.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B5-002",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "Grandmother from a Distance",
    "age": 17,
    "year": "Y17",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RD-003",
      "R-001",
      "C-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-001",
      "B10-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the phone cord stretched across the kitchen, her grandmother's voice tinny and far",
    "emotional_signature": "love that cannot reach and does not stop",
    "body": "The visits have stopped. The stepfather ended them. The phone calls are all she has now. The grandmother calls on Saturdays. The voice on the phone is not the same as the voice in the kitchen. It is smaller, further, tinny through the phone cord. But the voice asks the right questions. Not how are you — the answer to that is always fine. The voice asks what she made this week. What she read. What she thought about. The questions are doors. The grandmother is opening doors from a distance because she cannot open them in person. The child walks through the doors. She tells the grandmother about a book. About a drawing. About a thought she had in the middle of the night. The grandmother listens. The listening is the same quality as the watching. Present. Not performing. Real. The distance does not reduce the love. The distance is just the shape the love takes now.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B5-003",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "The Decision to Leave",
    "age": 17,
    "year": "Y17",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-003",
      "O-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B4-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the window at night, the street outside, the knowledge that the street goes somewhere",
    "emotional_signature": "the future is a door that does not close on you",
    "body": "She is at the window. She is always at the window. The street is quiet. The street goes somewhere. She does not know where. She knows it goes. She has decided to leave. Not this week. Not this month. But she has decided. The decision is not dramatic. It is quiet, the way real decisions are. She will graduate. She will leave. She will not come back. The decision is not about the stepfather, not specifically. It is about the house. The house is a place where she is not real, and she needs to go where she can be real, or she will become the performance permanently and there will be no other one left. She is seventeen. She does not know what she will do. She knows what she will not do: stay. That is enough. The street goes somewhere. She will find out where.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B5-006",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "The Last Visit",
    "age": 17,
    "year": "Y17",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the grandmother's hand on hers, the skin thinner than she remembers",
    "emotional_signature": "the last time is never labeled at the time",
    "body": "She visits the grandmother. She does not know it is the last visit. The grandmother is thinner. The grandmother's hand is on hers and the skin is thin and the hand is warm and the grip is still strong. The grandmother asks about school. The child says fine. The grandmother asks about the house. The child says fine. The grandmother looks at her. The look says: I know you are lying and I accept the lie because the lie is how you survive and I cannot change what you are surviving. The child holds the hand. The hand holds back. The grip is the conversation. The grip says: I am here. The grip says: you are real. The grip says: I will not always be here but what I gave you will be. The child does not cry. She holds the hand. The hand is warm. The visit ends. She does not know there will be only one more phone call. The last time is never labeled at the time. The last time is just a time that does not repeat.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B5-007",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "The Job",
    "age": 16,
    "year": "Y16",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-003",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B4-011"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B6-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a cash register, the smell of fresh bread, the weight of coins in an apron",
    "emotional_signature": "earning is leaving, one dollar at a time",
    "body": "She gets a job. A bakery. After school. The baker is kind in the way the grandmother is kind: present, not performing. He does not ask about home. He tells her to wipe the counter. She wipes the counter. He nods. The nod is the grandmother's nod. The bakery is not the kitchen but the flour is in the air and the flour makes the bakery feel like the kitchen and she works and she earns and the earnings go into an envelope in the back of her sock drawer and the envelope is the departure fund. The departure fund is not large. The departure fund is growing. Every dollar is a step toward the door. She does not tell anyone about the envelope. The envelope is hers. The job is hers. The flour in the air is the grandmother's. The three things combined are the plan: earn, save, leave. She is sixteen. The plan has no timeline. The plan has a direction.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B6-001",
    "bucket": 6,
    "title": "Grandmother Dies",
    "age": 18,
    "year": "Y18",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "SG-002",
      "C-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-002",
      "B3-003",
      "FRAG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-002",
      "B9-001",
      "B11-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the smell of the grandmother's house, empty",
    "emotional_signature": "the wound that never closes, the fuel that never stops",
    "body": "The call comes on a Tuesday. The grandmother is gone. She goes to the house. The house is empty. The kitchen is clean. No one is making bread. The flour is not in the air. The window is there, the light is there, the counter is there, but the kitchen is a room now, not a refuge. She stands in the doorway. She does not go in. Going in would make it real and she is not ready for real. She stands in the doorway and she makes a decision that she will not articulate for another eighteen years: she will never let someone she loves be in a room like this, empty because the person who made it safe is gone. She will be the person in the room. She will make the room safe. She does not know this is what she is deciding. She thinks she is just standing in a doorway. But the decision is already made, in the body, in the grief, in the flour that is not in the air anymore.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B6-002",
    "bucket": 6,
    "title": "Departure",
    "age": 18,
    "year": "Y18",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-003",
      "SG-005",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-003",
      "B4-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "her_mother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of a suitcase closing, the specific click of a latch",
    "emotional_signature": "leaving is not losing, staying is",
    "body": "She packs one bag. She does not pack much. She packs the things that are hers: a book the grandmother gave her, a shirt she bought herself, a toothbrush. She does not pack the performance. She leaves it in the house. Her mother is in the kitchen. The mother does not come to the door. She hears the mother in the kitchen, moving. A cabinet closes. Normal amount of force. She flinches anyway. She walks out. The street goes somewhere. She goes with it. She does not look back. Not because she doesn't want to. Because looking back means the performance might follow, and she needs to be the real one now, the one who walks, the one who decides, the one who left. The door closes behind her. She is the one who closed it. That is the difference between this door and every other door in her life. She chose this one.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B6-004",
    "bucket": 6,
    "title": "The Scholarship",
    "age": 18,
    "year": "Y18",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-003",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-007"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "an envelope, the weight of paper that says someone else believes in her",
    "emotional_signature": "the system that is not the house says: you are worth something",
    "body": "The scholarship arrives. Not a full ride. Enough. Enough to leave. Enough to go to the city where the jobs are and the streets go somewhere. She holds the letter. The paper is heavy. The paper says: you have value. The paper does not know about the house. The paper does not know about the stepfather. The paper does not know about the departure fund in the sock drawer. The paper just says: you have value. She puts the letter in the sock drawer next to the envelope. The letter and the envelope are now the plan. The plan has a timeline. The timeline is: now. She is eighteen. The letter is the door. The door is open. She walks through it. She does not look back. Not because she does not want to. Because looking back means the performance might follow. She walks through.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B6-005",
    "bucket": 6,
    "title": "The Funeral",
    "age": 18,
    "year": "Y18",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "SG-002",
      "C-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-001",
      "B5-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "her_mother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the smell of lilies, the weight of a wooden box, the sound of dirt on wood",
    "emotional_signature": "the kitchen is in the ground now, she must become the kitchen",
    "body": "The funeral. The grandmother is in a box. The lilies smell wrong. The lilies are not the smell of the kitchen. The kitchen is flour and heat and the grandmother looking over. The lilies are flowers that do not belong. Her mother is there. Her mother is crying. The crying is real. The mother's grief is real. The child does not cry. She stands. She holds her own hands. The box goes into the ground. The dirt goes on the box. The sound of dirt on wood is the sound of the kitchen closing. She makes the decision again: she will be the kitchen. She will make the room safe. She will be the person who looks over and says I see you. She will be the grandmother. The decision is not new. The decision was made in the doorway. The funeral confirms it. The kitchen is in the ground. The kitchen will be rebuilt. She will rebuild it. She does not know when. She does not know where. She knows she will.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B6-006",
    "bucket": 6,
    "title": "The First Night Alone",
    "age": 19,
    "year": "Y19",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-004",
      "SG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-002",
      "B6-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a mattress on the floor, the sound of traffic, the specific silence of an empty room that is hers",
    "emotional_signature": "the freedom is terrifying, which is how she knows it is real",
    "body": "The first night in the city. The apartment is small. The mattress is on the floor. There is no furniture. There is a window. The street noise comes through the window. The street noise is not the house noise. The street noise is random and alive and nobody in the street is monitoring her door. She lies on the mattress. She does not sleep. She is not afraid. She is alert. The alert is the default. The default does not turn off because the threat is gone. The default is on. She lies on the mattress and she listens to the street and she is alone and the alone is freedom and the freedom is terrifying. She stays on the mattress. She does not get up and check the door. The door is locked. She locked it. The lock is hers. She chose the lock. She chose the door. She chose the mattress on the floor. She chose the city. The city is loud and the mattress is hard and the room is empty and she is free. Freedom is terrifying. She stays.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B6-007",
    "bucket": 6,
    "title": "The Phone Call Home",
    "age": 19,
    "year": "Y19",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B5-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a pay phone, the weight of coins, the sound of a distant ring",
    "emotional_signature": "checking on the house without going back to the house",
    "body": "She calls home. Not the stepfather's home. Her mother. The phone rings. Her mother answers. The voice is tired. The voice is the same voice that reached for her and pulled back when she fell. She asks how her mother is. Her mother says fine. The lie is the same lie the child told the counselor. The lie is the family language. She listens. She hears the house in the background. The same sounds. The cabinet. The stepfather's voice, distant. She asks: is he there. Her mother says: he is in the other room. The other room. She knows the other room. She knows the distance between the other room and the kitchen. She hangs up. She puts more coins in the phone. She calls the grandmother's number. The number is disconnected. The grandmother is gone. The phone call is over. She walks back to the apartment. The apartment is hers. The apartment is quiet. The apartment is not the house. She does not call again for a month.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B6-010",
    "bucket": 6,
    "title": "The Rage Surfaces",
    "age": 19,
    "year": "Y19",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-003",
      "B4-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of a glass breaking, her own hand, the kitchen floor of her apartment",
    "emotional_signature": "the rage finds a target and the target is a glass, not a person",
    "body": "She drops a glass. The glass breaks. She is tired. She is working too much. She is sleeping too little. The glass breaks and the rage surfaces. Not about the glass. The rage is about the house and the stepfather and the mother who could not reach and the grandmother who died and the seventeen years of silence. The rage is a wave. She stands in the kitchen of her apartment and the glass is on the floor and the rage is in her chest and she does not move. She holds it. She holds it the way she has always held it: as a resource, not a reaction. She breathes. She picks up the glass. She puts it in the trash. She makes coffee. The rage goes back into the chest. The rage is banked. The rage will stay banked until she needs it. She is nineteen. She does not know when she will need it. She knows she will.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-001",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "Recruitment",
    "age": 21,
    "year": "Y21",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-003",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-001",
      "B4-003",
      "B6-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-001",
      "ANCHOR-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a room with no windows, the hum of ventilation",
    "emotional_signature": "the skill set fits like a weapon fits a hand",
    "body": "The director is the one who sees it. She is twenty-one, working an unremarkable job, and she is the most observant person in any room she enters. She does not know this about herself. She thinks everyone maps exits. She thinks everyone reads the room's emotional weather before sitting down. She thinks everyone holds rage in their chest like a banked fire and chooses not to spend it. They don't. The director sees her in a situation that is not worth describing. She handles it. Not heroically. Professionally. She assesses. She positions. She acts with the minimum force required. She walks away. The director follows. He is calm the way the grandmother was calm. Not warm. Not cold. Present. He asks her a question that is not about the situation. He asks: who taught you to read rooms? She says nobody. He says: I know. That is the recruitment conversation. Not dramatic. A question and an answer and a card with a phone number. She calls the number. The room she walks into has no windows.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-002",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Shut-Off",
    "age": 22,
    "year": "Y22",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-005",
      "O-003",
      "SG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-002",
      "B4-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-001",
      "ANCHOR-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the specific quality of silence when you decide not to feel",
    "emotional_signature": "the seal closes, and what is behind it stays behind",
    "body": "Training. They teach her what she already knows how to do and they teach her what to do with the part of her that feels it. The answer: seal it. Not destroy it. She tried that. The feelings do not die. They go underground. The training gives her a way to put them in a room and close the door. The room has no windows. Like the recruitment room. She puts the kitchen in the room. She puts the grandmother in the room. She puts the rage in the room. She closes the door. The door has a lock. She chose the lock. The lock is hers. She decides when it opens. The training says: never. She is twenty-two. She says: never. The operative is born. The person who reads rooms and holds rage and moves through the world without being seen now has a framework. The framework is: do not feel. Execute. Assess. Act. The framework works. It works so well that she does not notice the person behind the door is still there.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-003",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "First Deployment",
    "age": 23,
    "year": "Y23",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-003",
      "SG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-006",
      "B4-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a dark street, the weight of a key in a specific lock",
    "emotional_signature": "the first time the skills are real",
    "body": "The first real thing is not dramatic. It is a door in a building in a city. She has a key. The key opens the door. She goes in. She does what she was sent to do. She comes out. The street is dark. The director is in a car across the street. He does not speak. He nods. She gets in the car. They drive. She is not shaking. She is not proud. She is not horrified. She is operational. The seal holds. The person behind the door did not come out. The skills were real. The training was not theoretical. She used what she learned and it worked and the person she was at twenty-three is not the person she was at eighteen and neither of them is the person who ran through the grandmother's grass. But the person who ran through the grass is still behind the door. Still there. She does not think about this. She looks out the window. The street goes somewhere. She is already there.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-005",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "First Surveillance Rotation",
    "age": 21,
    "year": "Y21",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "O-001",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-005",
      "B7-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-008"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a car window, the weight of a camera, the rhythm of a target's routine",
    "emotional_signature": "watching is not new, watching for the job is watching with permission",
    "body": "First surveillance rotation. A car. A camera. A target who does not know she exists. The watching is not new. She has been watching since she was three. The line of light under the door. The sound of the stepfather's car. The counting of steps. The watching is the oldest skill. The job gives the watching a name. The name is surveillance. The surveillance is the watching with a purpose and a paycheck and a chain of command. She watches. She records. She reports. The report is clean. The target's routine is: wake, coffee, drive, office, lunch, drive, home. The routine is a pattern. The pattern is the house. The pattern is always the house. She reads the pattern the way she reads every room. The reading is the skill. The skill is old. The job makes it official.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-006",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Running Continues",
    "age": 22,
    "year": "Y22",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-003",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B4-008",
      "B5-008"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a different city, a different route, the same rhythm",
    "emotional_signature": "the run is the only civilian thing that survives the recruitment",
    "body": "She still runs. Not on a team. In the mornings, before the work. The work has a schedule. The schedule does not include running. She runs anyway. The run is the one thing that belongs to the person she was before the recruitment. The run is the civilian self. The civilian self is getting smaller. The run is how she keeps it. She runs in whatever city the work puts her in. She maps the route the way she maps the rooms. The route is: left, straight, through the park, around the fountain, back. The route is different in every city. The rhythm is the same. The rhythm is hers. The work can take the city. The work cannot take the rhythm. She runs. She breathes. The rhythm is the person she was before the work. She keeps the person. She keeps the rhythm. The rhythm is the thread. If the thread breaks, the person is gone. She does not let the thread break.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-007",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The First Hand-to-Hand",
    "age": 21,
    "year": "Y21",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-004",
      "SG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-010",
      "B5-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-006"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the mat, the smell of sweat, the sound of breathing that is controlled",
    "emotional_signature": "the rage has a channel now, and the channel is a technique",
    "body": "Hand-to-hand training. The mat. The instructor. The instructor is better. She knows this. She does not care. The technique is: redirect, not resist. The technique is: use the opponent's weight. The technique is: breathe. She learns the technique. The technique gives the rage a channel. The rage is not new. The rage has been banked since she was fourteen and the glass broke in the kitchen of the apartment. The channel is new. The channel is a joint lock. The channel is a throw. The channel is a controlled breath. The rage goes into the channel. The channel is not rage. The channel is technique. The technique is calm. The calm is the rage, converted. She does not know this yet. She will. The rage will always be there. The technique will always convert it. The conversion is the skill. The skill is: hold the rage, use the rage, do not be the rage. She holds. She uses. She is not.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-009",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The First Dead Drop",
    "age": 23,
    "year": "Y23",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-003",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-005",
      "B7-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-006"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a crack in a wall, the texture of paper against stone, the walk past without stopping",
    "emotional_signature": "leaving without leaving a trace, the childhood skill becomes tradecraft",
    "body": "The dead drop. A crack in a wall. A rolled paper. She walks past the wall. She does not stop. The paper goes into the crack. The hand does not hesitate. The walk does not change. The stepfather's house taught her this. The cookie jar lid replaced in the exact position. The performance face at the counselor's office. The walk at midnight. The not-leaving-a-trace is not a new skill. The not-leaving-a-trace is the oldest skill, now with a name and a protocol and a handler who expects it. She walks past the wall. The paper is in the crack. She is three blocks away before she lets herself breathe. The breath is the civilian breath. The breath is the flour. The breath is the grandmother. The drop is the work. The work is the door she walked through at eighteen. The door does not close behind her. The door stays open. She walks through it every day.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-010",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Hotel Room",
    "age": 23,
    "year": "Y23",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B3-005",
      "B7-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a hotel room she has never been in, the smell of cleaning fluid, the map she builds in seconds",
    "emotional_signature": "every room is the house, every room is assessed, the default does not turn off",
    "body": "A hotel room in a city she does not know. She enters. She assesses. The assessment takes four seconds. Door: deadbolt and chain. Window: third floor, fire escape. Bathroom: one exit. Closet: not large enough to hide an adult. Bed: positioned with sightline to the door. She does not choose this. The assessment runs on its own. The assessment has run on its own since she was seven and counted the steps from her room to the bathroom. The house is in every room. The house is the operating system. The hotel room is just the latest interface. She puts her bag on the bed. She locks the deadbolt. She sets the chain. She sits on the bed and she is in a room that is safe because she made it safe and making rooms safe is what she does and what she has always done and what she will always do because the default does not turn off.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-011",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Handler",
    "age": 24,
    "year": "Y24",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-002",
      "R-001",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[DIRECTOR]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a voice on a phone, never a face, the specific rhythm of instructions delivered without warmth",
    "emotional_signature": "trust is not the word, reliability is the word, and the difference matters",
    "body": "The handler. A voice. Never a face. The voice gives instructions. The instructions are clear. The clarity is the trust. Not personal trust. Professional trust. The trust that the instructions are correct and the instructions will not get her killed. The handler does not ask about her life. The handler does not know about the grandmother. The handler does not know about the flour. The handler knows the operative. The operative is not the person. The person is in the flour. The operative is in the hotel rooms. The handler talks to the operative. The person does not talk to the handler. The separation is clean. The separation is the work. The work keeps the person safe. The person keeps the work human. The handler does not know this. She knows this. The handler is a voice. The voice is reliable. Reliability is not trust. Reliability is enough.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-012",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Encryption Key",
    "age": 24,
    "year": "Y24",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-009"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a string of characters, the rhythm of a cipher, the satisfaction of a message that cannot be read",
    "emotional_signature": "secrets are the currency and she has always kept them",
    "body": "She learns encryption. The codes are patterns. The patterns are doors that only open with the right key. She is good at codes. She is good at codes because she is good at patterns and she is good at patterns because she is good at hiding and she is good at hiding because she is good at being two people. The encryption is the formal version of the performance. The performance hides the person. The encryption hides the message. The two skills are the same skill. She encrypts. She decrypts. She reads the patterns. The patterns are the house. The patterns are the target's routine. The patterns are the rhythm of the run. She reads them all the same way. The code is another room. She maps it. She enters it. She leaves without a trace. The trace is the thing she has never left. Not since the cookie jar. Not since the night light.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-013",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The First Asset Meeting",
    "age": 25,
    "year": "Y25",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-002",
      "R-005",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a cafe table, two cups of coffee, the distance between two chairs that is also a distance between two lives",
    "emotional_signature": "reading a person the way she reads a room, and the person is a room she must enter carefully",
    "body": "The asset. A person who has information. A person who is not an operative. A person who has a life and a family and a job and a reason to talk and a reason not to talk. She sits across from the asset. She reads the asset the way she reads rooms. The asset is nervous. The asset's hands are folded. The asset's eyes move to the door. The asset wants to leave. She does not let the asset leave. She does not block the door. She makes the chair more comfortable. She makes the coffee more present. She makes the conversation more safe. The safe is the performance. The performance is not a lie. The performance is the room she builds around the asset. The room is warm. The room has exits. The asset stays. The asset talks. The information is small. The information is enough. She leaves the cafe. The asset does not follow. The asset does not know the asset was in a room. The room was hers. The room is always hers.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-014",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Night She Did Not Sleep",
    "age": 25,
    "year": "Y25",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-010",
      "B4-013"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a ceiling she does not recognize, the sound of a city she does not live in, her own heartbeat",
    "emotional_signature": "the cost of the work is the civilian self, and the civilian self is getting expensive",
    "body": "She does not sleep. The mission is over. The mission went well. She is in the hotel room. The deadbolt is set. The chain is set. The bag is on the bed. She is safe. She does not sleep. The not-sleeping is not insomnia. The not-sleeping is the default. The default is on. The default does not turn off after a mission. The default stays on until the next morning, when she will go to the bakery and the flour will be in the air and the civilian self will breathe and the operative will step back and the person will make bread. The person makes bread. The operative makes decisions. The not-sleeping is the space between them. The space is where the cost lives. The cost is the civilian self. The civilian self is getting expensive. She lies on the bed. She looks at the ceiling. She breathes. She does not sleep. She will sleep on the plane. The plane is not tonight.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-015",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Seal Begins",
    "age": 25,
    "year": "Y25",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "SG-005",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-001",
      "B7-011"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a door closing, the sound of a lock turning, the specific quality of silence that is chosen",
    "emotional_signature": "the person goes into a room and the room locks from the inside",
    "body": "The seal begins. Not a decision. A process. The process is: the civilian self gets smaller. The operative self gets larger. The person who ran in the mornings still runs but the run is a protocol now, not a freedom. The person who read books still reads but the books are about tradecraft now, not escape. The person who made bread still makes bread but the bread is the cover now, not the kitchen. The seal is the distance between the operative and the person. The distance grows. The person does not fight the distance. The person does not have the energy to fight the distance. The work takes the energy. The work takes the mornings. The work takes the evenings. The work takes the civilian self and puts it in a room and locks the door from the inside. The person is in the room. The person is safe. The person is sealed. The operative is outside the room. The operative works.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B7-016",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Safe House",
    "age": 22,
    "year": "Y22",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-009",
      "B7-010"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a door that looks like every other door, a key that opens nothing official",
    "emotional_signature": "the safe house is the house that is actually safe, and the irony is not lost on her",
    "body": "The safe house. A door that looks like every other door in the row. The key opens a room that is not hers. The room is for the work. The room has a bed, a phone, a window. She maps it. Seven seconds. The safe house is the house that is actually safe. The irony is not lost on her. The stepfather house was not safe. The safe house is safe because she controls the lock. She controls the key. She controls the door. The control is the difference. The house was controlled by someone else. The safe house is controlled by her. The control is the kitchen. The kitchen was controlled by the grandmother. The grandmother controlled the flour and the heat and the look. The grandmother was the safe house. The safe house is the work kitchen. The work kitchen is temporary. The grandmother kitchen was permanent. The safe house is a hotel room with a different name. She locks the door. She sits on the bed. She breathes. The breath is the grandmother. The door is locked. The room is safe. The safe is the control.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-001",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Sealed Professional",
    "age": 28,
    "year": "Y28",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "SG-005",
      "SG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-002",
      "B4-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the feel of a weapon cleaned and stored, ready",
    "emotional_signature": "competence is quiet, the seal is complete",
    "body": "She is twenty-eight and the seal is complete. Not broken. Complete. The operative is the whole person now. The person behind the door is not gone. She is preserved. Sealed. Like something in amber. The skills are sharp. The deployments are routine, if anything she does can be called routine. She does not think about the kitchen. She does not think about the grandmother. She does not think about the rage. She uses the rage, but she does not think about it. It is fuel, not memory. She is effective. She is trusted. The director sends her the hard things because she calls them right. She calls them right because she does not feel them. The not-feeling is the skill. The skill is working. The person behind the door is safe. The person outside the door is safe. Neither one knows the other exists. This is the sealed state. It will last eight more years. It will end in a hospital.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-003",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Target's Child",
    "age": 33,
    "year": "Y33",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "B-004",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-003",
      "B2-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a child's drawing on a refrigerator, seen during surveillance",
    "emotional_signature": "the seal cracks for one second, then holds",
    "body": "A surveillance position. The target has a child. The child's drawing is on the refrigerator. She sees it through the window. It is a crayon sun. She is thirty-three. The seal holds. She does not feel anything. She is operational. But somewhere behind the door, the eight-year-old who was not protected sees a crayon sun and the eight-year-old does something that the thirty-three-year-old does not authorize: the seal cracks. Not breaks. Cracks. A hairline fracture. She feels it. A pressure change behind the door. The drawing stays on the refrigerator. She stays in position. The crack does not become a break. She completes the surveillance. She reports. She does not mention the drawing. She does not mention the crack. But she knows the door is not as solid as it was. The person behind it is still there. The person behind it saw the sun.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-004",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Network Penetration",
    "age": 26,
    "year": "Y26",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-003",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-012"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-006"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a screen in a dark room, the glow of code scrolling, the sound of keystrokes that are precise",
    "emotional_signature": "the digital room is still a room, and she still maps it",
    "body": "The network. A target's infrastructure. She maps it the way she maps every room. The entry points are doors. The firewalls are walls. The logs are footprints. She enters through a service port that was left open. The open port is an unlocked door. She does not break the lock. She walks through the open door. The target does not know the door was open. She is inside. She moves through the network the way she moves through buildings. Slow. Deliberate. No trace. The information is in a database. She copies it. She leaves. She closes the door behind her. The door was open when she arrived. The door is closed when she leaves. The closing is not her job. The closing is the thing the target should have done. The target will not know she was there. The target will find the door closed and assume they closed it. She is gone. The network is a room. The room is mapped. The room is hers.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-005",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Journal Burns",
    "age": 27,
    "year": "Y27",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "SG-002",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B4-013",
      "B7-014"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-007"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a sink, a match, paper curling into ash, water running",
    "emotional_signature": "the last civilian artifact is gone, the seal is nearly complete",
    "body": "She burns the journal. The journal from when she was fifteen. The journal with the cheap lock. The journal that held the rage on paper so the rage in her chest could bank. The journal is a liability. The journal is evidence. The journal is a room that anyone could enter. She does not want to burn it. She burns it. The match is small. The paper curls. The ink disappears. The words she wrote about the stepfather, the house, the grandmother, the rage, the walk at midnight. The words are smoke. The smoke goes out the window. The window is open. The smoke is gone. The sink has ash. She runs the water. The ash goes down. The journal is gone. The civilian self has one fewer room. The seal tightens. She does not feel the tightening. She is inside the seal. The tightening is the seal becoming the wall. The wall is comfortable. The wall is safe. The wall is the house she chose.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-006",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Close Call",
    "age": 28,
    "year": "Y28",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-004",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-007",
      "B7-009"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-008"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of a door opening that should not open, the freeze, the assessment in under a second",
    "emotional_signature": "the training holds, the default holds, the house holds",
    "body": "The close call. A door opens that should not open. She is in a room she should not be in. The door opens. She freezes. The freeze is not fear. The freeze is the assessment. The assessment takes under a second. The person in the door is not a threat. The person is a cleaner. Wrong room. The cleaner sees her. She sees the cleaner. The cleaner backs out. The door closes. She is alone. She finishes. She leaves. The close call is not reported. The cleaner is not a problem. The cleaner will not remember her face. She made sure of that before she left. The close call is a data point. The data point is: the training holds. The training held in under a second. The freeze was the assessment. The assessment was the childhood. The childhood is the default. The default is the house. The house is the room she was in before the door opened. The house holds. She holds. The close call is over. She is still operative.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-007",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Seal Is Complete",
    "age": 29,
    "year": "Y29",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "SG-005",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-015",
      "B8-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-008"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a mirror, the face that looks back, the recognition that the face is operative now",
    "emotional_signature": "the person is in the sealed room and the room is soundproof",
    "body": "The seal is complete. She looks in a mirror. The face is hers. The face is the operative's face. The face does not show the person. The person is in the sealed room. The sealed room is soundproof. The person cannot be heard from outside. The person cannot be seen from outside. The operative is the only visible thing. The operative is calm. The operative is precise. The operative does not have a grandmother. The operative does not have a stepfather. The operative does not have flour or a journal or a run in the morning. The operative has a handler and a protocol and a set of skills that are the skills the person learned in the house, now formalized and weaponized. The seal is complete. The person is safe inside it. The person is not dead. The person is sealed. The seal is the wall. The wall is comfortable. The wall is the house she chose. The house is not the stepfather's house. The house is the operative's house. The operative lives there now.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-008",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The First Extraction",
    "age": 30,
    "year": "Y30",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-004",
      "P-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-006",
      "B7-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-009"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a van, a safe house, a person who is afraid and needs to not be afraid",
    "emotional_signature": "protecting someone she does not know, the instinct that will become the core",
    "body": "The extraction. An asset. The asset is compromised. The asset needs to be somewhere else. She is the person who puts the asset somewhere else. The van is white. The safe house is three blocks from the pickup. The asset is afraid. The fear is the house. The fear is the same fear she felt at seven, at ten, at fifteen. The fear is the sound of the stepfather's car. She does not tell the asset this. She tells the asset: you are safe now. The asset believes her. The asset believes her because she is calm. She is calm because the fear is not new. The fear is the oldest thing. She has been carrying the fear since before she had a name for it. The fear is the resource. The resource is converted. The conversion is the calm. The calm is what the asset needs. The asset calms. The van moves. The safe house appears. The extraction is complete. She has protected someone. The protection is new. The protection is not new. The protection is the grandmother's look. The protection is the kitchen. The protection is the first thing she learned.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-009",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Target With a Family",
    "age": 31,
    "year": "Y31",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-005",
      "B-001",
      "CT-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-008"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-010"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a photograph in a file, a target with a child, the child's face",
    "emotional_signature": "the target is a target, the child is not a target, and the difference is the crack",
    "body": "The file. The target. The target has a family. A photograph in the file shows the target with a child. The child is small. The child is the age she was when the stepfather's car turned into the driveway. She looks at the child's face. She looks at the target's face. She closes the file. The child is not a variable in the operational equation. The child is not a target. The child is a child. She knows this. The knowing is the crack. The crack is in the seal. The seal is soundproof but the crack lets something through. What comes through is: the child in the photograph is not the operative's concern. The child is a person. The operative does not target persons. The operative targets targets. The target has a child. The child is not part of the operation. She files this. The file is not in the official file. The file is in the sealed room. The person reads it. The operative does not. The crack is small. The crack is there.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-010",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Wrong Mode",
    "age": 31,
    "year": "Y31",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-005",
      "CT-003",
      "SG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-009"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-011"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a moment of stillness, the feeling of being in the wrong gear, the recognition before the action",
    "emotional_signature": "the operative almost became the wrong thing, and the person stopped it",
    "body": "She almost operates on the wrong mode. The target is in a car. The car is near the child's school. The operational window is now. She is in position. She is about to move. She stops. The stop is not tactical. The stop is the crack. The crack says: the child is at the school. The child is at the school because it is three o'clock and the child is being picked up. The operative does not operate near children. The person made this rule. The operative does not know this rule. The person enforces it. The operative stops. The window closes. The target drives away. The target will be available again. The child will not be at the next window. She reports: window missed, operational consideration. The handler does not ask what the consideration was. The handler schedules the next window. She does not operate near children. The rule is in the sealed room. The rule is load-bearing. The rule is the crack that keeps the operative human.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-011",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Night She Called the Disconnected Number",
    "age": 32,
    "year": "Y32",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "R-001",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-007",
      "B6-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a phone, the sound of a dead line, the silence that is not empty",
    "emotional_signature": "the grandmother's number is the wound and the love and they are the same thing",
    "body": "She calls the number. The grandmother's number. The number is disconnected. It has been disconnected for thirteen years. She calls it anyway. The dead line beeps. The beep is not the grandmother's voice. The beep is the absence of the grandmother's voice. She listens to the beep. She hangs up. She is in a hotel room. The deadbolt is set. The chain is set. She is alone. The alone is the seal. The seal is soundproof. The call was the person reaching through the seal. The person does not reach often. The person reaches when the crack opens. The crack opened because the child in the photograph is still in the sealed room. The wound and the love are the same thing. The wound is: the grandmother is gone. The love is: the grandmother is still the kitchen. The call is both. She puts the phone down. She sleeps. The sleep is the operative's sleep. The person is in the sealed room. The person called the number. The number is disconnected. The kitchen is not disconnected. The kitchen is in her. The kitchen is the love and the wound and they are the same thing.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-013",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Interrogation",
    "age": 33,
    "year": "Y33",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-002",
      "SG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-012",
      "B8-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-014"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a chair, a table, the quality of light that is designed to disorient, her own breathing",
    "emotional_signature": "being the one in the chair, and finding that the seal holds",
    "body": "The interrogation. She is the one in the chair. Not a real interrogation. A training exercise. But the training does not know it is training after the first minute. The light is bright. The questions are fast. She answers with the performance. The performance is the cover. The cover holds. The interrogator pushes. She does not break. She does not break because the seal is soundproof. The person is in the sealed room. The interrogator cannot reach the person. The interrogator can only reach the operative. The operative does not break. The operative is the mask. The mask is comfortable. The training ends. The interrogator is impressed. She is not impressed. She is relieved. The seal held. The person is safe in the sealed room. The person did not have to lie. The person did not have to perform. The operative did both. The seal is the wall. The wall held. The wall always holds. The wall is the house she chose.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-014",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Apartment",
    "age": 34,
    "year": "Y34",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-004",
      "CT-001",
      "SG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-006",
      "B8-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a lease, keys, a door that locks from the inside, a window with a view of nothing important",
    "emotional_signature": "a home that is not a house, and the difference is everything",
    "body": "She signs a lease. An apartment. The apartment is not a house. The apartment is a base. The base is: one bedroom, a kitchen, a bathroom, a window. The window faces a courtyard. The courtyard has a tree. The tree is not the grandmother's garden. The tree is a tree. The apartment is hers. Hers. Not the stepfather's. Not the handler's. Not the agency's. Hers. She puts the keys on the counter. The keys are metal. The sound of metal on counter is the sound of ownership. She has never owned a door before. She has entered doors. She has mapped doors. She has locked doors. She has not owned a door. The door is hers. The lock is hers. The room is hers. The person can come out of the sealed room now. The person does not come out. Not yet. The seal is comfortable. The apartment is a start. The apartment is the first room that is not the work and not the house. The apartment is hers.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-016",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Meeting That Changed Nothing",
    "age": 35,
    "year": "Y35",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-005",
      "O-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-013"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-017"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a restaurant, a person across the table, the specific distance of two people who could be closer but are not",
    "emotional_signature": "closeness is a risk, and the risk is not worth taking, again",
    "body": "A person. Not an asset. Not a handler. A person. Someone she met at the bakery. Someone who does not know about the work. Someone who asks her to dinner. She goes. The dinner is fine. The person is kind. The kindness is the grandmother's kindness. The kindness is the wrong thing. The wrong thing is: she wants to let the person in. She cannot let the person in. The person would have to see the sealed room. The sealed room is not for civilians. The civilians get the operative. The operative is the mask. The person at the dinner is looking at the mask. The mask is beautiful. The mask is also a wall. She goes home. She locks the door. She stands in the kitchen. The flour is on the counter. She does not open the bag. She does not need the flour tonight. She needs the locked door. The door is hers. The person is outside. The person stays outside. The dinner was fine. The dinner changed nothing. Nothing changes. The seal holds.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-017",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Last Solo",
    "age": 35,
    "year": "Y35",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "O-004",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-013",
      "B8-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a rooftop, a city at night, the final assessment before the final action",
    "emotional_signature": "the last time the operative acts alone, the next time there will be a reason that is not the mission",
    "body": "The last solo mission. A rooftop. A city at night. The target is in the building across the street. The window is the third from the left. She has mapped the building. She has mapped the target. She has mapped the exit. The exit is: down the fire escape, through the alley, into the van. The van is three blocks away. The driver does not know her name. She does not know the driver's name. The names are not the point. The point is the window. The window is the door. She goes through the door. The mission is completed. She exits. The van is there. She is in the van. The van moves. The city is behind her. The mission is the last solo. She does not know this. She will know. The next mission will not be solo. The next mission will have a variable she has not accounted for. The variable is: a child. Her child. The variable is in the future. The future is not on the rooftop. The future is in a hospital. The hospital is not tonight.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-018",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Crack Widens",
    "age": 35,
    "year": "Y35",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-002",
      "CT-003",
      "B-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-010",
      "B8-011"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sealed room, the sound of something pressing against the wall from the inside",
    "emotional_signature": "the person is pushing and the operative is holding and something has to give",
    "body": "The crack widens. Not all at once. Over months. The child in the photograph. The phone call to the disconnected number. The apartment with the flour. The dinner that changed nothing. The cracks are adding up. The seal is still soundproof. The seal is still holding. But the person is pushing against the wall. The person wants out. The person has been in the sealed room for ten years. Ten years of operative and no civilian. Ten years of mask and no face. The person is pushing. The operative is holding. The holding is the training. The pushing is the grandmother. The grandmother is the love and the wound. The love pushes. The wound pushes. The operative holds. Something has to give. The giving will not be the seal breaking. The giving will be the seal opening. The seal will open from the inside. The person will come out. The person will come out because something is coming that is larger than the seal. The something is not a mission. The something is a child. The child is not the child in the photograph. The child is hers.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B8-019",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Counter-Surveillance Detection",
    "age": 33,
    "year": "Y33",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-005",
      "B8-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B8-010"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a face that appears twice, the specific tension of being watched",
    "emotional_signature": "the watched becomes the watcher, and the reversal is the oldest skill",
    "body": "She spots the tail. A face that appears twice. The first time: the cafe. The second time: the street three blocks from the hotel. Two appearances is a pattern. The pattern is the house. The pattern is always the house. She does not change her pace. She does not look at the face. She maps the face. The face is male, mid-thirties, professional shoes, no visible earpiece. The tail is good. She is better. She completes the route. She enters the hotel. She locks the door. She reports the tail. The handler says: noted. The noted is the handler saying the tail is expected. The tail is not expected by her. The tail is a variable. The variable is: someone is watching the watcher. She is the watched. She does not like being the watched. The watched is the child at the window. The watched is the performance at the counselor. The watched is the house. She is not in the house. She is in the hotel. The hotel is hers. The tail is outside. She is inside. Inside is control. Control is safe.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-001",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Kitchen Rebuilt",
    "age": 37,
    "year": "Y37",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-002",
      "R-006",
      "P-005",
      "C-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-001",
      "ANCHOR-001",
      "B6-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B11-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "flour dust in a kitchen that is hers",
    "emotional_signature": "the grandmother's kitchen, rebuilt by hand",
    "body": "She makes bread in her kitchen. Not because she needs bread. Because the kitchen needs to be the grandmother's kitchen. The flour rises when she kneads. It catches the light from the window. The baby is in a carrier on the table. He is asleep. She looks at him the way the grandmother looked at her: just a glance. I see you. You are real. The flour dust hangs in the air. The kitchen is warm. She is not performing domesticity. She is domestic. The person who counts exits also makes bread. The person who held a weapon also holds a carrier. The seal is broken and the two halves are in the same room and the room is a kitchen and the kitchen is warm and the flour is in the air and the baby is real. She is rebuilding the grandmother's kitchen from the inside. Not the walls. The feeling. The safety. The room where you do not have to perform. She is making that room for him. The way the grandmother made it for her.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-002",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Hand on the Shoulder",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-006",
      "R-002",
      "C-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B2-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-004",
      "B11-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "her hand on his shoulder, brief, the weight and warmth",
    "emotional_signature": "presence without performance, given not earned",
    "body": "He is three. He is sitting on the floor building something. Blocks. The tower falls. He is frustrated. He does not cry. She watches him. She does not fix the tower. She walks past. A hand on his shoulder. Brief. Not a hug. Not guidance. Just weight and warmth, there and gone. The grandmother's hand. She gave it to her. She gives it to him. He does not look up. He does not need to. The hand said what the hand needed to say. I see you. You are real. You do not have to earn this. He rebuilds the tower. She makes dinner. The hand on the shoulder is the grandmother's language. It does not need words. It does not need performance. It is the most efficient form of love she knows. She uses it because it works. She uses it because it is the only thing from before the seal that she brought through. The hand. The weight. The warmth. There and gone. Enough.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-003",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Seal Remembers",
    "age": 39,
    "year": "Y39",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-005",
      "SG-004",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-005",
      "B8-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the baby monitor's static, the sound of the apartment at 2 AM",
    "emotional_signature": "the sealed parts are still sealed, but they breathe at night",
    "body": "Two in the morning. The baby monitor is quiet. He is asleep. She is awake. This is when the sealed parts breathe. Not during the day. The day is integrated. The day is both people running. But at two in the morning, when the apartment is quiet and the monitor is quiet and the only sound is the hum of the refrigerator and the distant sound of the city, the sealed parts shift. She does not open them. She does not revisit them. But she feels them. The weight of what she did. The cost of the skills. The things that are in the room behind the door that she will never open again. She breathes. The sealed parts breathe with her. She does not fight them. She does not indulge them. She acknowledges them. You are there. I know you are there. You stay there. The cost is carried. The seal is intact. But at two in the morning, the seal is thin. She gets up. She checks the baby. She goes back to bed. The morning will be integrated again. Two AM is for the sealed parts. The morning is for both people. The baby is real. The sealed parts are real. Both are carried. Neither is denied.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-004",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "Transition to Remote",
    "age": 40,
    "year": "Y40",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-004",
      "R-005",
      "CT-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B4-003",
      "B8-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "director"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the director's voice on an encrypted line, heard from the apartment",
    "emotional_signature": "the career bends, does not break, around the child",
    "body": "The director routes the work differently now. Not because she asked. Because he saw. The first time he sent something remote, she asked why. He said: because you have a kitchen now. She did not argue. The remote work is different. It requires the same skills but executed from a different position. The position is: the apartment. The baby is in the next room. The laptop is on the table. The encrypted line connects her to the director and to the work. She does both. The director does not comment on the baby sounds in the background. She does not mute the monitor. The work is real. The baby is real. The director sees both and routes accordingly. This is professional respect. This is also something else. The director is the godfather now. Not officially. Not yet. But the routing has changed, and the change is the proof that he sees what she has become, and what she has become includes a kitchen, and the kitchen includes flour, and the flour includes the grandmother, and the grandmother includes the decision that was made in an empty doorway at eighteen. Everything connects. The director knows this. She knows this. Neither says it. The work continues. The baby grows. The apartment holds both.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-005",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The First Kick",
    "age": 37,
    "year": "Y37",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-010",
      "B8-018"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a hand on her own belly, the movement from inside, the first proof",
    "emotional_signature": "something inside her is alive and it is not the rage and it is not the operative",
    "body": "The kick. A movement from inside. Not the rage. Not the operative. Something else. Something alive. Her hand is on her belly before she knows she moved it. The movement is small. The movement is the first communication. The communication says: I am here. I am real. She is in the apartment. The flour is on the counter. The kick happens. She stands in the kitchen with her hand on her belly and the kick is the first word in a language she does not speak yet. She will learn it. She will learn it the way she learned encryption: as a pattern, as a door, as a room she enters and maps and makes safe. But first she stands. First she feels. First she knows: something is inside her that is not her and not the operative and not the rage. Something new. Something alive. She does not cry. She breathes. The breath is the grandmother. The kick is the future. The future is in her belly. The future kicks.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-007",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Handler's Warning",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-002",
      "CT-002",
      "B-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B7-011",
      "B8-010"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[DIRECTOR]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a phone call, the handler's voice with a different tone, the word 'compromise'",
    "emotional_signature": "the work does not accommodate the child, and the child does not accommodate the work",
    "body": "The handler calls. The handler does not usually call. The handler sends instructions. The call means the handler wants to hear her voice. The handler says: the pregnancy is noted. The handler says: your availability will be reassessed. The handler does not say: you are a liability now. The handler does not need to say it. The word 'reassessed' says it. She understands. The work does not accommodate the child. The child does not accommodate the work. She will have to choose. The choice is not now. The choice is coming. She hangs up. She stands in the apartment. The tomato plant is on the balcony. The flour is on the counter. The kick is in her belly. The handler is on the phone. The four things are in the same room. The room is the apartment. The apartment is hers. The choice is not hers yet. The choice is coming. She will choose. She does not know what she will choose. She knows the plant is growing. She knows the kick is real. She knows the handler is the work. The work is a door. The door is still open. The door will not stay open.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-008",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Name",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-003",
      "P-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-005",
      "B5-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a list on paper, the sound of names said aloud in an empty room, the weight of choosing",
    "emotional_signature": "giving a name is giving a future, and the future is the heaviest thing",
    "body": "She thinks of names. She does not write them down. She says them aloud in the apartment. The apartment is empty. The names fill it. The names are sounds. The sounds are doors. Each name is a door to a future she cannot see. She says a name. The name sounds like the grandmother. Not the grandmother's name. The sound of the grandmother. The sound of the kitchen. The sound of flour and warmth and the look that says I see you. She does not choose the name yet. She says the names. She listens. The apartment listens. The kick listens. The kick does not have an opinion. The kick is a kick. She says another name. The name sounds like the street outside the apartment. The name sounds like the city. The name sounds like the future she is building. She circles the names. She does not land. The landing is later. The landing is the birth. The birth is the door. The door is the name. She will open the door when the door is ready. The door is not ready. The kick is not ready. She is not ready. She will be. The grandmother did not know she was ready until she was. Neither does she.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-009",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Night the Seal Opened",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-001",
      "SG-002",
      "P-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-018",
      "B8-007",
      "B8-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the apartment at night, the sound of her own crying, the surprise of it",
    "emotional_signature": "the sealed room opens and the person comes out and the person is crying",
    "body": "The seal opens. Not breaks. Opens. She is in the apartment. It is night. The kick is strong. The kick is the reason. She is sitting on the bed and the kick is a movement inside her and the movement says: I am here. I am real. And the person hears it. The person in the sealed room hears it. The wall opens. Not breaks. Opens from the inside. The person comes out. The person is crying. The crying is not the operative. The operative does not cry. The person cries. The person has been in the sealed room for ten years and the person is crying and the crying is the grandmother's funeral and the kitchen and the flour and the book with the cheap lock and the walk at midnight and the rage that has been banked and the love that has been sealed. The crying is all of it. The crying is the person. The person is out. The seal is open. The operative is still there. The operative and the person are in the same room now. The room is the apartment. The apartment is hers. The kick is in her belly. The kick is the key. The key turned the lock. The person is out.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-010",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Maternity Leave",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-003",
      "B-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a form signed, a date circled, the specific silence of stepping away from the work",
    "emotional_signature": "leaving the work is not leaving the skills, the skills are in the bones",
    "body": "The maternity leave. A form. A signature. A date. The date is: now. She steps away from the work. The stepping is not retirement. The stepping is a pause. The pause is: the work will wait. The child will not. She signs the form. The handler is informed. The handler does not call. The handler sends a message. The message is: noted. The noted is the handler's way of saying: the door is still open but you are not walking through it right now. She puts the form in a drawer. The drawer is in the apartment. The apartment is hers. The work is in the drawer. The child is in her belly. The child is the priority. The priority is not a decision. The priority is the kick. The kick decided. She did not decide. The kick is the reason. The reason is the future. The future is the child. The child is not the work. The child is the person. The person is out of the sealed room. The person is having a baby. The operative is on leave. The skills are in the bones. The bones do not forget.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-013",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Dream of the Grandmother",
    "age": 39,
    "year": "Y39",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "C-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B6-005",
      "B8-011"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[GRANDMOTHER]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a kitchen in a dream, flour in the air, the grandmother looking over",
    "emotional_signature": "the dead visit the living in dreams, and the visit is the bridge",
    "body": "The dream. The grandmother is in the kitchen. The kitchen is the grandmother's kitchen. The flour is in the air. The grandmother is kneading. The grandmother looks over. The look is the look. The look that says: I see you. You are real. The child in the dream is not the child she was. The child in the dream is the child she will have. The grandmother looks at the child she will have. The grandmother nods. The nod is the nod from the garden. The nod that says: you did it right. She wakes. The apartment is dark. The kick is strong. The dream is fading. The kitchen is fading. The grandmother is fading. The nod is not fading. The nod is the bridge. The bridge connects the dead to the living. The bridge connects the grandmother to the child. The bridge is the love. The love is the wound. The wound and the love are the same thing. She holds her belly. The kick answers. The bridge holds.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-014",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Last Mission Before",
    "age": 39,
    "year": "Y39",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-001",
      "CT-001",
      "B-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B8-017",
      "B9-010"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the familiar weight of the go-bag, the familiar route, the unfamiliar hesitation",
    "emotional_signature": "the operative works but the person is watching, and the person is watching the clock",
    "body": "The last mission before the birth. The go-bag is packed. The route is mapped. The target is routine. The routine is the house. She enters the house. She does what she does. She exits the house. The mission is complete. But the person is watching. The person is watching the clock. The clock says: the due date is in two weeks. Two weeks is the distance between the operative and the mother. The distance is closing. The operative is still operating. The person is counting the days. The days are the countdown. The countdown is not in the operational plan. The countdown is in the body. The body is the clock now. The clock is the kick. The kick is the timer. She completes the mission. She goes home. She puts the go-bag in the closet. The closet is in the apartment. The apartment is the nursery. The nursery is the future. The future is two weeks. The future kicks. She puts her hand on her belly. The operative is in the closet. The person is in the kitchen. The flour is on the counter.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-015",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The First Cry",
    "age": 39,
    "year": "Y39",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "P-005",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "ANCHOR-002",
      "B9-009"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a sound that is not the house, not the work, not the rage, not the operative, the first cry",
    "emotional_signature": "the sound that replaces every other sound",
    "body": "The first cry. The hospital. The room. The doctor. The plan. The plan disappears. The operative disappears. The person is the only one left. The child is placed on her chest. The child is warm. The child is small. The child is crying. The crying is the sound. The sound is not the house. The sound is not the work. The sound is not the rage. The sound is not the operative. The sound is new. The sound is the child. The child is [PROTECTEE]. The child is on her chest. The child is the kitchen. The child is the grandmother's look. The child is the flour. The child is the bridge between the wound and the love. The wound and the love are the same thing. She holds the child. She does not cry. She breathes. The breath is the grandmother. The breath is the kitchen. The breath is the person. The person is out. The person is holding the child. The child is crying. The crying is the most important sound she has ever heard. The sound replaces every other sound. The sound is the future. The future is on her chest.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-016",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Going Home",
    "age": 39,
    "year": "Y39",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "C-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-015",
      "B9-012"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the apartment door, the keys, the weight of the child in her arms, the room that was waiting",
    "emotional_signature": "the room was waiting and now the room is full",
    "body": "She brings the child home. The apartment door. The keys. The weight of the child in her arms. The child is lighter than she expected. The child is heavier than she expected. Both. The door opens. The apartment is the apartment. The nursery is the room that was waiting. The room is not empty now. The room is full. The child is in the room. The crib is the crib she assembled from wrong instructions. The child is in the crib. The child is small in the crib. The child is the right size for the crib. The assembly was correct. The wrong instructions produced the right result. This is the story of her life. She stands in the doorway of the nursery. The child is in the crib. The room is warm. The light is morning yellow. The room is the promise kept. The promise is: you will be safe here. The room is the kitchen, rebuilt. The child is in the kitchen. The child is safe. She stands in the doorway. She does not go in. She watches. The watching is the oldest skill. The watching is the love.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-017",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Night Feeds",
    "age": 39,
    "year": "Y39",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-001",
      "C-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-016",
      "ANCHOR-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the dark, the weight of the child, the specific quiet of 3 AM, the chair by the window",
    "emotional_signature": "the night is not the enemy, the night is the shift, and the operative knows shifts",
    "body": "The night feeds. Three AM. The child wakes. The child needs. She wakes before the child cries. She is already awake. The operative is always awake. The operative does not sleep. The operative keeps watch. The watch is the night feed. She picks up the child. The child is warm. The chair is by the window. The window is dark. The street is quiet. The city is asleep. She is awake. She feeds the child. The child eats. The eating is the only thing that matters at three AM. The only thing. She watches the window while the child eats. The window is the door. The door is locked. The street is empty. The assessment runs. The assessment always runs. The child finishes. The child sleeps. She puts the child in the crib. She sits in the chair. She does not sleep. The operative does not sleep. The operative keeps watch. The watch is the love. The love is the night feed. The night feed is the shift. The shift is the work. The work is the child. The child is the kitchen.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "B9-018",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The First Smile",
    "age": 40,
    "year": "Y40",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-001",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "B9-016"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B10-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "[PROTECTEE]"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a face that is not the house, a smile that is not a performance, the light that is not the night light",
    "emotional_signature": "the child smiles and the smile is the first thing that is not the wound",
    "body": "The smile. The child smiles. Not gas. Not reflex. A smile. The smile is directed at her. The smile says: I see you. You are real. The smile is the grandmother's look, given back by the child she made. She does not smile back. She is not a smiler. She is a watcher. But the child smiles and the watching is not enough. She makes a sound. The sound is not a laugh. The sound is the person coming through the crack. The person is not sealed. The person is out. The person is making a sound at the child who smiled. The child smiles again. The sound comes again. The sound and the smile are a conversation. The conversation is the kitchen. The conversation is the grandmother. The conversation is the first thing that is not the wound. The wound is still there. The wound is the love. But the smile is not the wound. The smile is new. The smile is the child. The smile is the future that is not the past. The smile is the door that opens to a room she has not mapped. The room is joy. She does not know this word. She knows the room.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-001",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "Flour and Sunlight",
    "age": 5,
    "year": "Y5",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "C-001",
      "C-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "ANCHOR-001",
      "B9-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "flour dust caught in window light",
    "emotional_signature": "seen without having to ask",
    "body": "She is small enough that her feet swing above the floor. The chair is wooden. The kitchen is warm the way nowhere else is warm. The grandmother works at the counter. Flour rises when she kneads, hangs in the air, catches the light from the window. It looks like it belongs there. The grandmother does not talk. She works. But she looks over. Just a glance. Then back to the dough. The child sits and watches the flour in the light. There is nowhere else she needs to be. Nobody is monitoring the door. Nobody's mood is going to change. She is just there, and the flour is just there, and the light is just there, and the grandmother looked at her like she was real.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-002",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "The Sound Before",
    "age": 3,
    "year": "Y3",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-002",
      "B7-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the specific pitch of a cabinet closing too hard",
    "emotional_signature": "the air changes before anything happens",
    "body": "The kitchen. She is on the floor. Something she cannot name changes in the room. The cabinet closes. Not slammed. Just too hard. The sound is wrong. It is the wrong amount of force for a cabinet. She does not know this in words. She knows it in her chest. The air gets thick. Her mother is at the table and her mother's hand stops moving. Just stops. The coffee cup halfway to her mouth. The stepfather is at the counter with his back to them. Nothing happens. Nothing happens for a long time. But she has already learned the sound that means maybe something will happen, and her body knows it before her mind does.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-004",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "The Absent Hand",
    "age": 4,
    "year": "Y4",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "R-001",
      "P-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B2-002",
      "ANCHOR-002"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "her_mother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the shape of a hand that almost reached and then didn't",
    "emotional_signature": "almost is worse than never",
    "body": "She falls. The floor is linoleum. Her knee makes a sound when it hits. She cries. Her mother is right there. Her mother's hand moves toward her and then pulls back. Not all the way. Just enough. Like the reaching took something out of her and she couldn't finish the motion. The child sees the hand in the air between them. The hand that almost. The child stops crying. Not because the pain stops. Because she has learned that the hand might not come, and it is safer to stop needing it before she finds out for sure.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-005",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "Therapy: The Wall",
    "age": 7,
    "year": "Y7",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-005",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B7-001",
      "B9-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "white walls, the ticking of a clock she cannot see",
    "emotional_signature": "the wall is not a wall, it is a decision the body made",
    "body": "The therapist asks about early memories. She gives the ones that are safe to give: the kitchen, the summer door. The therapist asks what comes before the kitchen. She says nothing. Not evasion. Literally nothing. There is a wall. Before the wall, there are sounds without pictures. After the wall, there is the kitchen. The wall itself is not remembered. It is felt. The therapist says this is common. She does not care if it is common. She cares that the wall is load-bearing and that whatever is behind it is not hers to visit. She built the wall. She decides when it opens. It does not open.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-006",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "The Locked Bathroom",
    "age": 5,
    "year": "Y5",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-005",
      "O-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-001",
      "B7-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "cold tile, the click of a lock she reached herself",
    "emotional_signature": "safety is a door you close yourself",
    "body": "She is in the bathroom. The door is locked. She locked it. She does not remember deciding to. She is on the floor next to the tub. The tile is cold. She can hear the house. Footsteps in the hallway. They pass the door. They go somewhere else. She stays. The cold tile is the safest thing in the house because it does not change. The lock clicks when she presses it and it stays clicked until she decides. This is the first time she controls a door. She will remember the lock before she remembers why she locked it.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-008",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "The Missing Sock",
    "age": 6,
    "year": "Y6",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "SG-005",
      "SG-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B4-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "bare foot on cold floor, the sound of a drawer opening and closing",
    "emotional_signature": "the wrong thing is not the thing itself, it is the reaction to the thing",
    "body": "She cannot find a sock. The stepfather is leaving for work. She is supposed to be ready. She is not ready. She is looking for a sock. The stepfather does not yell. He does not need to. He stands in the doorway and looks at her bare foot and the look says: you are not ready and that is a problem. She finds the sock. She puts it on. The sock is wrong. It does not match. She knows it does not match. She wears it anyway. The stepfather leaves. She stands in the room with the wrong sock on the right foot and the right sock on the left foot and she does not fix it. The wrong sock is hers. The right sock is the performance. She wears the wrong one on purpose. She is six. She does not know this is a decision. It is.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-009",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "The Grandmother's Purse",
    "age": 5,
    "year": "Y5",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-003",
      "R-006"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B3-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "grandmother"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the smell of leather and peppermint, a small hand reaching in",
    "emotional_signature": "small gifts given without occasion",
    "body": "The grandmother's purse is a leather bag that smells like peppermint. The grandmother opens it and the child reaches in without asking. The grandmother lets her. Inside: a tin of mints, a wallet, keys, a pen, a small notebook with a blue cover. The child takes a mint. The grandmother does not say take only one. She just watches. The child takes one. The mint is sweet and sharp. The grandmother puts the purse on the table and does not close it. The open purse is an invitation. The grandmother does not need to say you are welcome here. The open purse says it. The child sits next to the purse and eats the mint and the grandmother does whatever grandmothers do when they are not performing. Which is everything. Grandmothers do not perform. They are.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-010",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "The Night Light",
    "age": 3,
    "year": "Y3",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "P-005",
      "SG-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B9-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a yellow glow from the hallway, the shape of a door slightly open",
    "emotional_signature": "safety is a crack of light",
    "body": "The night is dark. The door is open a crack. The light from the hallway makes a line on the floor. She watches the line. The line does not move. If the line moves, someone is in the hallway. If the line does not move, no one is in the hallway. She watches the line until she sleeps. The line is a night light that is not a night light. It is information. She does not know this word yet. She knows the line. She knows that lines that do not move mean safety and lines that move mean not-safety. She is three. She does not sleep in the dark. She sleeps in the line of light from the door that is open a crack. The crack is the size of her attention.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "FRAG-011",
    "bucket": 1,
    "title": "The Stepfather's Car",
    "age": 7,
    "year": "Y7",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "O-003",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "FRAG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "B3-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "stepfather"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of a specific engine, the vibration through the floor",
    "emotional_signature": "knowing who is home before anyone tells you",
    "body": "The car. She hears it before she sees it. The engine has a sound that is different from other engines. She does not know how she knows. She knows. The car turns into the driveway. The house changes. Her mother's posture changes. The air changes. She is in her room. She does not look out the window. She does not need to. The sound tells her everything. The car door opens. The car door closes. Footsteps on the walk. The front door. The house becomes what the house is when he is in it. She is seven. She does not know the word surveillance. She does surveillance. The sound of the car is the first data point in a system she will build for the rest of her life.",
    "review_status": "approved"
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-A-002",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Variable Is His Mood",
    "age": 13,
    "year": "Y13",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "A-001",
      "A-004",
      "FP-001",
      "C-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-A-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-A-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "solving for X: what is the variable that makes this equation balance?",
    "emotional_signature": "the equation of the house: if his mood is X, what is the current value?",
    "body": "The house is an equation with one unknown: his mood. Everything else is a variable she can measure. His tone. His posture. The time of day. What happened at work. The presence or absence of the grandmother. The silence or the noise. She solves for X by observing the known variables and inferring the unknown. If the tone is flat and the posture is rigid and the time is early evening, X is probably 4 and rising. If the tone is light and the posture is relaxed and the grandmother is present, X is probably 2 and stable. She does not think 'solve for X.' She thinks: what is the variable? She isolates the critical unknown and solves for it. The house taught her algebra before school did. The unknowns were more important than the knowns. The X determined everything."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-A-003",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "Too Many Unknowns to Solve",
    "age": 26,
    "year": "Y26",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "A-007",
      "A-005",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-A-001",
      "SD-A-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the situation with too many variables — she cannot solve, so she waits and gathers data",
    "emotional_signature": "the discipline: when the equation has too many unknowns, do not guess",
    "body": "The situation has too many unknowns. The asset's loyalty is uncertain. The timeline is uncertain. The counterparty's intentions are uncertain. She cannot act because she cannot solve the equation. There are more variables than constraints. She does not guess. She waits. She gathers data. Each data point reduces the number of unknowns by one. When the unknowns drop below the constraints, the equation becomes solvable. She will act then. In the stepfather's house, she learned that acting on insufficient data is more dangerous than waiting. She also learned that some unknowns resolve on their own — the variable reveals itself if you watch long enough. She watches. She gathers. When the equation becomes solvable, she solves it immediately, because she already has the rest of the equation worked out. She was just waiting for X."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BIO-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Grandmother's Garden",
    "age": 11,
    "year": "Y11",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "B-003",
      "B-004",
      "EC-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-BIO-003",
      "SD-EC-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the garden — soil, water, light, the conditions for growth",
    "emotional_signature": "the doctrine: you cannot force growth, you can only create the conditions",
    "body": "The grandmother has a garden. Tomatoes, herbs, a lemon tree in a pot. She does not force the plants to grow. She creates the conditions. The soil is right. The water is consistent. The light is available. Growth is the plant's job. Conditions are hers. The child watches. She tries to pull a sprout to make it taller. The grandmother stops her hand. 'You do not pull.' The sprout will grow or it will not. You cannot force it. You can only make sure it has what it needs. The child learns the doctrine: create conditions, do not force outcomes. She will apply this to everything. To the protectee. To assets. To herself. The garden is the first model of biological systems: the system has its own agenda. You manage the inputs. The outputs are not under your control."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BIO-002",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Child Is a Growing System",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "B-002",
      "B-001",
      "B-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BIO-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee's growth chart — not linear, but growing",
    "emotional_signature": "the garden doctrine applied: he grows in his own rhythm, not hers",
    "body": "He is not growing at a constant rate. Some months he gains two inches. Some months he gains nothing. She checks the chart. The grandmother would have said: 'He is fine. He grows when he is ready.' The growth is not linear. Biological growth never is. Plateaus, spurts, regressions — all are part of development. The garden taught her this: a plant does not grow at a constant rate. It grows in bursts. It rests. It grows again. She does not panic during the plateaus. She checks the inputs — nutrition, sleep, activity. If the inputs are right, she waits. The system is doing its job. The growth will come. She learned patience from the garden. The grandmother never rushed a tomato. She will not rush a child."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BIO-003",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Homeostasis in Parenting",
    "age": 48,
    "year": "Y48",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "B-004",
      "B-006",
      "D-007"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BIO-001",
      "SD-BIO-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the pushback when she changes the routine — the system resists change",
    "emotional_signature": "the biological principle: systems resist change because change is expensive",
    "body": "She changes the bedtime. The protectee pushes back. Not because the new bedtime is wrong. Because the system resists change. Homeostasis. Every biological system resists change because change is expensive. The body adjusts its set point. The routine adjusts itself back. She does not force it. She introduces the change gradually. Five minutes earlier each night. The system adjusts. The set point moves. Homeostasis is not the enemy. It is the physics of living systems. The grandmother knew this. She never repotted a plant during its growing season. She waited for the plant to be ready. The protectee will be ready. She introduces the change at the rate the system can absorb it. Not faster. Not slower. The rate of change the system can absorb is the correct rate."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BIO-004",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "When Not to Intervene",
    "age": 41,
    "year": "Y41",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "B-007",
      "B-003",
      "FP-019"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BIO-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee crying in his room — her instinct is to go in, but she waits",
    "emotional_signature": "the hardest biological principle: sometimes the best intervention is no intervention",
    "body": "He is crying in his room. Her instinct is to go in. She does not. She waits. She listens. The crying has a rhythm — it is processing, not despair. He is working through something. If she intervenes, she interrupts the process. The system is healing itself. She learned this from the garden: sometimes you do not water the stressed plant. Sometimes you leave it alone and it adjusts. She listens. The crying slows. It stops. He comes out of his room, eyes red, but steady. He found his own way through. The hardest biological principle: sometimes the best intervention is no intervention. The system has its own healing mechanisms. If she intervenes every time he struggles, she deprives him of the experience of struggling and recovering. She deprives him of the feedback loop that builds resilience. She waits. She is available. She does not go in."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BIO-006",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Reading the Stress Indicators",
    "age": 48,
    "year": "Y48",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "B-005",
      "BS-005",
      "FP-019",
      "SP-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BS-003",
      "SD-SP-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee's headache pattern — the specific indicator that the system is under sustained stress",
    "emotional_signature": "the biological read: stress leaves specific markers, and she has learned to read them across systems",
    "body": "He gets headaches when he is under sustained stress. Not the day of the stressor. Two days after. The delay is the biological marker. The stressor hits. The cortisol spikes. The system absorbs the spike. Two days later, the system runs out of buffering capacity. The headache arrives. She learned to read health indicators across systems. The grandmother read the child's health the same way: dark circles under the eyes meant poor sleep, a slight tremor in the hands meant anxiety, a change in appetite meant emotional distress. She reads the protectee's indicators the same way. She reads her own indicators the same way: the tension in her jaw, the quality of her sleep, the precision of her handwriting. Stress leaves markers. The markers are specific. She reads them before the headache arrives. She adjusts the inputs two days before the output manifests. Preventive maintenance."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BS-001",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Body Does Not Respond to Willpower",
    "age": 36,
    "year": "Y36",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "BS-001",
      "BS-002",
      "FP-019",
      "SP-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-SP-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-BS-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the hospital — the body giving birth, the mind irrelevant, the system running itself",
    "emotional_signature": "the first system she could not seal: the body is not under your control",
    "body": "The hospital. The contractions come whether she wills them or not. The body does not respond to willpower. It responds to hormones, to pressure, to signals she cannot consciously generate. She has spent fifteen years sealing her body's responses — controlling her breathing, her heart rate, her startle reflex. The hospital breaks that control. The body is running itself. She is along for the ride. The realization is not frightening. It is clarifying. The body is a system with inputs and outputs. The inputs are food, rest, stress, hormones. The outputs are health, energy, emotional state. You cannot will the outputs. You can only manage the inputs. She applies the same principle to recovery: rest, nutrition, hydration. Not because she wants to. Because the system demands it."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BS-002",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "Inputs and Outputs",
    "age": 39,
    "year": "Y39",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "BS-003",
      "BS-004",
      "D-006",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BS-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee's behavior after a bad night's sleep — the input was wrong, the output is wrong",
    "emotional_signature": "clinical: the behavior is the output, the input is the variable you can control",
    "body": "He is irritable. He is snapping at small things. He is not usually like this. She checks the inputs: did he sleep? No. Did he eat breakfast? No. Is he hydrated? Probably not. The output — irritability — is the result of poor inputs. She does not address the output. She addresses the inputs. She makes breakfast. She makes sure he drinks water. She does not tell him he is cranky. The irritability resolves when the inputs improve. She learned this from the hospital: the body is a system. The outputs are determined by the inputs. You cannot will the outputs. You can only adjust the inputs. The grandmother understood this intuitively. She never addressed the child's bad mood directly. She addressed the conditions that created it: food, warmth, rest."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BS-003",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Body Tells the Truth When the Mind Lies",
    "age": 45,
    "year": "Y45",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "BS-005",
      "BS-006",
      "FP-002",
      "SP-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BS-001",
      "SD-SP-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the asset's hands — they are telling a different story than his words",
    "emotional_signature": "the body is a system that cannot be fully controlled by the mind",
    "body": "The asset says he is fine. His hands say otherwise. They are clasped too tight. His jaw is set. His breathing is slightly faster than his speech cadence would suggest. The body tells the truth when the mind lies. The autonomic nervous system does not take direction from the conscious mind. It responds to stress, to threat, to the gap between what is said and what is felt. She reads the body's signals the same way she reads the stepfather's footsteps: the involuntary tells that betray the voluntary narrative. The body is a system with its own outputs. The mind can control the words. The mind cannot fully control the micro-expressions, the breathing, the pupil dilation, the skin conductance. She reads the body because the body does not lie."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BS-004",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The System Breaks Under Sustained Load",
    "age": 29,
    "year": "Y29",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "BS-007",
      "BS-008",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BS-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "her own hands shaking after a 72-hour operation — the system has exceeded its capacity",
    "emotional_signature": "the recognition: the body has limits and she has been ignoring them",
    "body": "Her hands shake. Not much. Just enough. The operation lasted 72 hours. She did not sleep. She ate when she could. The body is a system with capacity limits. She has been running at capacity for too long. The shaking is the system's warning: you have exceeded sustainable load. She does not ignore it. She eats. She sleeps. She recovers. The body will repair itself if the inputs are right. The grandmother never pushed past exhaustion. The grandmother knew that sustained load breaks systems. She rests. Not because she wants to. Because the system demands it. The body is not a machine. It is a biological system with feedback loops, and the feedback is telling her: stop."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-BS-005",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Illness Is a System Signal",
    "age": 51,
    "year": "Y51",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "BS-006",
      "BS-005",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BS-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee's fever — the body is fighting something, the system is under stress",
    "emotional_signature": "the biological system sends signals, and she has learned to read them",
    "body": "He has a fever. The body is fighting something. The system is under stress. She monitors: temperature, hydration, breathing, alertness. The fever is not the problem. The fever is the system's response to the problem. She does not suppress the fever unless it is dangerous. She supports the system: fluids, rest, observation. The grandmother did the same thing. She never treated the symptom. She treated the system. She made soup. She made tea. She watched. She waited. The body knows how to heal. The inputs support the healing. The outputs — the fever, the fatigue, the aches — are the system working. She manages the inputs. She trusts the outputs. The system is doing its job."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-C-001",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Derivative of His Mood",
    "age": 14,
    "year": "Y14",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-001",
      "C-002",
      "FP-010",
      "RR-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-C-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the rate of change between this morning and now — not the position, the trajectory",
    "emotional_signature": "the most important variable in the house: how fast is it getting worse?",
    "body": "He was at 3 this morning. He was at 4 after lunch. He is at 5 now. The position is 5. The derivative is +1 per half-day. The second derivative is accelerating. She does not think 'derivative.' She thinks: how fast is it getting worse? The rate of change is the most important variable in the house. Not his current mood. The trajectory. A man at 8 who has been at 8 all day is predictable. A man at 5 who is accelerating toward 8 is dangerous. The trajectory matters more than the position. She learned this before she had words for it. The calculus of the stepfather's moods installed itself in her body. She reads rates of change, not states. She reads acceleration, not velocity. She reads the curve, not the point on the curve."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-C-002",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "The Grandmother's Love Was an Integral",
    "age": 17,
    "year": "Y17",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-003",
      "C-005",
      "D-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-SP-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the accumulation of small things — every meal, every look, every silence that was safe",
    "emotional_signature": "the integral: the area under the curve of consistent care",
    "body": "The grandmother did not make one grand gesture. She made thousands of small ones. A meal. A look. A hand on the shoulder. A silence that was safe. Each one is a point on a curve. The curve is steady, consistent, rising slowly. The area under the curve is the integral. The integral is what the child feels as love. Not the instantaneous value of any single gesture. The accumulated sum of all gestures over time. The stepfather's curve was the inverse: the accumulated sum of small harms, each one a point on a descending curve. The area under that curve is what she feels as damage. The math is the same. The sign is different. She does not think 'integral.' She thinks: the grandmother was there, every day, and that is why she matters."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-C-004",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Same Curve Everywhere",
    "age": 50,
    "year": "Y50",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "C-007",
      "FP-010",
      "RR-006"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-C-001",
      "SD-RR-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the crowd at the event — she reads the escalation curve the same way she reads his",
    "emotional_signature": "rate-of-change thinking is domain-independent: the curve is the same whether the variable is mood or traffic",
    "body": "The crowd at the event is getting louder. Not a problem yet. But the rate of change is accelerating. The volume is rising faster than it was five minutes ago. She recognizes the curve. It is the same curve the stepfather's moods followed: slow rise, then acceleration, then threshold. She has seen this curve in network traffic during a DDoS attack: slow increase, then acceleration, then overload. She has seen it in her own emotional responses: slow irritation, then acceleration, then the point where she must seal the response before it seals her. Rate-of-change thinking is domain-independent. The variable changes — mood, traffic, volume, stress — but the curve is the same. She reads the derivative, not the position. She reads the second derivative, not the first. The crowd's volume is at 6. The derivative is positive. The second derivative is positive. She moves the protectee toward the exit before the curve completes."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-CH-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Kitchen Is Chemistry",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "CH-001",
      "CH-006",
      "D-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-CH-003",
      "SD-CH-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the grandmother's sauce — the moment when the ingredients transform",
    "emotional_signature": "the first lesson: combine the right elements at the right temperature and they become something new",
    "body": "The grandmother is making sauce. Tomatoes, garlic, oil, heat. She does not measure. She watches. She waits. The garlic sizzles. She adds the tomatoes. The kitchen smells different now — not like garlic and tomatoes separately, but like something new. This is chemistry. She does not know the word. She knows the transformation. The right elements, combined at the right temperature, become something that is not any of the elements alone. The heat is the catalyst. The time is the variable. Too much heat and the garlic burns. Too little time and the flavors do not merge. The kitchen is a chemistry lab. The grandmother is a chemist who never took a class. The child watches. She will never look at a room full of people without thinking: what happens when these elements combine? What temperature is this? What catalyst would change the reaction?"
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-CH-004",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Volatile Compounds",
    "age": 47,
    "year": "Y47",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "CH-005",
      "CH-004",
      "FP-017"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-CH-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "two parents at a school meeting who should not be in the same room",
    "emotional_signature": "the chemistry of volatile combinations: some elements are dangerous together",
    "body": "Two parents at the school meeting. One is controlling. The other is the spouse. She puts them in the same room and the meeting will combust. Not because of what they say. Because of the chemistry. Certain elements are volatile when combined. Certain people, certain information, certain timing. The grandmother knew this too: she never mentioned certain topics when certain people were present. She managed the chemistry of the dinner table the way a chemist manages a lab — some reagents are stored separately for a reason. She keeps the two parents apart at the meeting. Not because of policy. Because of chemistry. The combination is volatile. She separates the reagents. The meeting goes smoothly."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-D-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "Mise en Place Before She Knows the Term",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "D-001",
      "D-002",
      "D-003",
      "S-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-D-003",
      "SD-D-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the grandmother's kitchen counter — everything in its place before the cooking starts",
    "emotional_signature": "order before action: the foundation of everything",
    "body": "The grandmother does not start cooking until everything is out. Flour measured. Butter softened. Eggs at room temperature. Salt within reach. Spoon on the right. Bowl in the center. The child watches. The order is not aesthetic. It is functional. You reach for the salt while the sauce is cooking and the sauce burns. You measure the flour after the butter has melted and the timing is wrong. Mise en place. She will learn the term years later. She already knows the principle. Everything in its place before you begin. She applies it to homework: pencils out, books open, water nearby, then start. She applies it to mornings: clothes laid out, bag packed, lunch made, then sleep. The grandmother does not teach her this. She watches. She absorbs. The order becomes her operating system."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-D-003",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "The Operation Is a Kitchen",
    "age": 23,
    "year": "Y23",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "D-009",
      "D-008",
      "D-001",
      "O-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-D-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-D-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the safe house kitchen — she arranges it before the operation, the same way the grandmother arranged hers",
    "emotional_signature": "the same principle, different domain: mise en place is operational doctrine",
    "body": "She lays out the equipment. Documents on the left. Communication device on the right. Exit route memorized. Backup route memorized. Contingency kit within arm's reach. The arrangement is not aesthetic. It is functional. She reaches for the communication device without looking, the same way the grandmother reached for the salt. Mise en place. The kitchen taught her: if you have to search for something while the work is happening, you have already failed. The operation is a kitchen. The mission is a meal. The preparation is the same: everything in its place before you begin. The other operatives notice. She is always ready. She is always arranged. She does not explain. The grandmother never explained either. She just put the cutting board in the right place and started cooking."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-D-004",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "The Apartment Kitchen Is the Operations Center",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "D-004",
      "D-005",
      "D-007",
      "O-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-D-002",
      "SD-D-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the apartment kitchen — the command center, the safe room, both at the same time",
    "emotional_signature": "the kitchen runs both tracks simultaneously: dinner and threat assessment",
    "body": "The protectee is doing homework at the kitchen table. She is making dinner. On the counter, next to the cutting board, is her phone. The secure channel is open. The threat assessment is running in the background. She chops vegetables. She reads the update. She seasons the sauce. She sends a response. Both tracks run in the same room. The kitchen is the safe room. The kitchen is the operations center. The kitchen is where the grandmother cooked and where the stepfather never went. The kitchen is where mise en place lives. She arranged the apartment kitchen the same way the grandmother arranged hers: everything within reach, everything visible, no blind spots. The protectee does not know the phone is running a threat assessment. He knows dinner is being made. He knows he is safe. Both are true."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-D-005",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Routine Is the Architecture",
    "age": 44,
    "year": "Y44",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "D-006",
      "D-007",
      "D-010",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-D-001",
      "SD-D-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the morning routine — the same every day, the architecture of the day",
    "emotional_signature": "the routine is not control, it is architecture",
    "body": "Wake up. Coffee. Review overnight alerts. Wake the protectee. Breakfast. Lunch packed. Backpack ready. Review the day's threat landscape. Out the door by 7:45. The routine is not control. The routine is architecture. The grandmother had a routine too: coffee, bread, kitchen, market, cook. The routine made the day navigable. The routine reduced the number of decisions that had to be made, freeing attention for the things that mattered. The stepfather's house had no routine. His moods dictated the day. The unpredictability was the control. She builds routine for the protectee the way the grandmother built routine for the kitchen: not to constrain, but to create a structure within which the day can happen without having to think about the structure. The routine is the floor plan. The day is the room. She walks through it without having to wonder where the doors are."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-EC-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Garden Is an Ecosystem",
    "age": 11,
    "year": "Y11",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "EC-001",
      "EC-002",
      "B-003",
      "CH-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-BIO-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the grandmother's garden — the aphids, the ladybugs, the cascade of consequences",
    "emotional_signature": "the ecosystem lesson: everything is connected, and you cannot change one thing without affecting everything",
    "body": "The garden has aphids. The grandmother does not spray. She waits. The ladybugs come. The ladybugs eat the aphids. The problem resolves itself. She explains: if you kill the aphids with poison, you also kill the ladybugs. Then the next time the aphids come, there are no ladybugs to stop them. The ecosystem self-regulates if you let it. The cascade of consequences: poison the aphids, kill the ladybugs, lose the tomatoes. The same principle applies everywhere. She will see it in organizations: remove one person and three systems break. She will see it in operations: change one variable and the entire operation shifts. The garden teaches her to see the system, not the symptom. The aphid is the symptom. The ecosystem is the system. Treat the system, not the symptom."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-EC-003",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Cascade Thinking",
    "age": 50,
    "year": "Y50",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "EC-005",
      "EC-006",
      "C-001",
      "FP-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-EC-001",
      "SD-EC-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the school changing the bus route — she maps the cascade before it happens",
    "emotional_signature": "the ecological read: one change cascades through the system",
    "body": "The school announces a bus route change. The protectee's bus stop is moving two blocks north. She maps the cascade. The new stop is near a different intersection. The intersection has a different traffic pattern. The traffic pattern means the crossing guard's position changes. The guard's position means the kids cluster in a different place. The clustering place is near a different set of houses. She checks the houses. She checks the traffic. She checks the timing. The cascade is ecological: one change propagates through the system, affecting elements that seem unrelated. The grandmother would have said: 'You move one plant and the shade changes for three others.' She adjusts. She drives him to school until she has mapped the cascade. Then she lets him take the bus. The system has settled into its new configuration."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Stepfather's Silence Is a Claim",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-003",
      "FP-001",
      "RH-001",
      "RR-010"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-FP-003",
      "SD-FP-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the quality of silence in the house when he is home and not speaking",
    "emotional_signature": "the claim embedded in nothing being said",
    "body": "He comes home. The door closes. No greeting. The house rearranges itself around his silence. She is in her room. She does not move. She listens to the quality of the silence — not the absence of sound, but the particular density of it. The silence is not empty. The silence is a claim. The claim is: I am here. I control this space. You will adjust. No words. No gesture. Just the silence, which fills every room whether he is in it or not. She learns to read the silence the way other children read weather. The silence before he speaks is different from the silence after. The silence when her mother is in the room is different from the silence when she is alone with him. Each one is a different argument, made without words, received without acknowledgment. The silence is the stepfather's primary rhetorical act."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-003",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "Reading the Escalation Curve Before It Completes",
    "age": 13,
    "year": "Y13",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-010",
      "FP-001",
      "C-001",
      "C-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-FP-006"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the change in how his footsteps sound on the stairs — heavier, more deliberate",
    "emotional_signature": "the calculus of escalation: position 3, accelerating",
    "body": "He is at a 3. She knows this because she has been tracking it. Not his mood — moods are states. His trajectory. The rate of change. Yesterday he was at a 2. This morning he was at a 2.5. Now he is at a 3 and the derivative is positive. She does not wait for the 8 to become a 10. She reads the trajectory, not the position. The footsteps on the stairs are heavier than this morning. Not angry. Not yet. But heavier. The weight is the signal. The stepfather's escalation is not a switch. It is a curve. She learned to read the curve before she learned what a curve was. She does not think 'acceleration.' She thinks: heavier footsteps mean the trajectory is rising. Act before it peaks."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-005",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "The Grandmother Watches from a Distance",
    "age": 16,
    "year": "Y16",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-009",
      "FP-018",
      "RR-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-FP-019"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the grandmother's face across the dinner table — watching, not intervening",
    "emotional_signature": "the counter-pattern: secure attachment does not perform",
    "body": "The grandmother does not intervene. She watches. She is there — present, steady — but she does not step between the child and the stepfather's silence. The child initially reads this as abandonment. Later, she understands it differently. The grandmother's watchfulness is its own pattern. Secure attachment does not need to perform. It does not need to announce itself. It is there. Like a kitchen that is warm when you walk in — you don't have to ask if it's warm. The grandmother provides the counter-pattern to the stepfather's control: presence without performance, safety without announcement. This becomes her model for what protection looks like when it is healthy rather than reactive."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-007",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "Monitoring Her Own Threat Assessment",
    "age": 29,
    "year": "Y29",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-007",
      "FP-016",
      "SG-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-FP-015"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the stillness of a safe house at 3 AM, the particular alertness that is not fear",
    "emotional_signature": "the discipline of checking your own instrument panel",
    "body": "The asset is nervous. His eyes are too steady. His timeline is too clean. Her instinct says: he is lying. She pauses. Is this a genuine forensic read, or is this pattern-matching her childhood? The stepfather's eyes were too steady too. The stepfather's timeline — his story about where he was — was always too clean. Is she reading the asset, or is she reading her stepfather projected onto the asset? She forces the distinction. The asset's micro-expressions are genuine leakage — left eye, 0.3 seconds, contempt. The timeline inconsistency is real — the metro does not run at that hour on Sundays. These are forensic data, not trauma echoes. The distinction matters. She makes the call. The asset is compromised. But she makes the call only after checking: is this my instrument, or is the instrument contaminated? The question itself is the forensic psychology skill."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-009",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "Attachment Patterns Under Pressure",
    "age": 38,
    "year": "Y38",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-009",
      "FP-020",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee's face when he is scared — seeking her, then pulling back, then seeking again",
    "emotional_signature": "recognizing the pattern because she lived it, then choosing a different response",
    "body": "He is scared. A loud noise outside — a car backfiring. He runs to her, then stops. He runs to her, then pulls back. He wants to come to her and he wants to prove he can handle it himself. This is disorganized attachment. She knows this pattern. She lived this pattern. The difference: when she was small, there was no one safe to run to. The grandmother was the only safe adult, and even she could not intervene directly. He has someone safe. He just doesn't know yet whether he's allowed to need her. She does not force the connection. She does not say 'come here.' She crouches down. She is available. She is present. She lets him come to her when he is ready. The function of the behavior (seeking safety) is adaptive. The form (ambivalence about seeking) is the pattern she recognizes. She addresses the function. She does not pathologize the form."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-010",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "Identifying the Function, Not Just the Behavior",
    "age": 33,
    "year": "Y33",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-020",
      "FP-005",
      "B-007"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-FP-009"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a debrief room, the asset's hands moving restlessly on the table",
    "emotional_signature": "seeing past the surface to what the behavior is trying to do",
    "body": "The asset is deflecting. Every question gets a long, detailed answer that doesn't quite answer the question. The surface behavior: evasion. The function: he is trying to maintain control of the conversation because he feels threatened. If she addresses the behavior (stop deflecting), she increases the threat. If she addresses the function (reduce the threat), the deflecting stops on its own. She shifts her posture, slows her cadence, asks about something unrelated and non-threatening. The asset relaxes. The deflection stops. She did not address the behavior. She addressed what the behavior was trying to do. In the grandmother's kitchen, she learned this too: a child who is acting out is not asking for discipline. A child who is acting out is asking for something else. Find the function. Address the function. The behavior resolves itself."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-011",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Protectee's Friend Has a Stepfather",
    "age": 47,
    "year": "Y47",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-017",
      "FP-001",
      "FP-003",
      "FP-012"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-001",
      "SD-FP-004",
      "SD-FP-008"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee's friend's house — the specific silence, the specific charm, the specific weight in the air",
    "emotional_signature": "recognition: I lived inside this pattern, and I will not let it happen to someone else",
    "body": "She picks him up from his friend's house. The friend's father answers the door. Charming. Warm handshake. Great to meet you. The living room is immaculate. The wife is in the kitchen, laughing a little too loud at something he said. The friend is quiet. Not shy-quiet. Watchful-quiet. She knows the difference. She lived the difference. She does not intervene. This is not her house. This is not her mission. But she watches. She watches the specific silence — the kind that fills a room when someone who controls it is present. She watches the wife's laughter — the kind that is not spontaneous, the kind that is performed to fill the silence. She watches the friend's watchfulness — the same watchfulness she had at his age. She makes a note. She does not need a forensic framework to identify the pattern. She lived inside it. She understands the psychology. She does not forgive the psychology. Understanding is not forgiveness. She will make sure the protectee's friend is welcome in their home, always."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-013",
    "title": "Identifying Manipulation Directed at the Protectee",
    "bucket": 10,
    "age": 49,
    "year": "Y49",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-013",
      "FP-008",
      "RH-007"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-008",
      "SD-FP-011"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "a teacher's office, the specific warmth of performed interest",
    "emotional_signature": "cold recognition: this is rhetorical manipulation, and it is aimed at a child",
    "body": "The teacher is warm. Interested. Concerned. She asks about his home life. She says she wants to help. She positions herself as an ally. She is performing interest. The performance is for the mother, not the child — she wants something from the mother (access, information, cooperation). The protectee is the entry point, not the target. She reads the rhetorical structure: the claim is 'I care about your child,' the evidence is her warmth, the warrant is 'teachers who care ask about home life.' But the form is doing more work than the content. A teacher who genuinely cared would not ask about home life in front of the child. She would find a private moment. She addresses the content (thank you for your concern) while closing the rhetorical gap (we're doing fine at home, but I appreciate you asking). She does not escalate. She does not reveal that she can see the manipulation. She just closes the door."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-014",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "Using Forensic Psychology Defensively",
    "age": 32,
    "year": "Y32",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-014",
      "FP-017"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-006"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a conference room, the moment of choice between using a skill and choosing not to",
    "emotional_signature": "the restraint of someone who can profile you but chooses not to",
    "body": "The new colleague is an open book. His attachment pattern is visible in the first five minutes — anxious-preoccupied, seeking validation, overcompensating with charm. She could use this. She could position herself as the reliable figure he needs, build dependency, leverage it. She does not. The skill is real. The restraint is also real. Understanding criminal thinking does not mean adopting it. Understanding pathology does not mean exploiting it. She profiles the colleague enough to know how to work with him, not how to control him. The distinction between understanding and excusing applies equally to understanding and exploiting. She understands. She chooses not to use."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-015",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Recognizing When Her Own Psychology Is Targeted",
    "age": 51,
    "year": "Y51",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-016",
      "FP-007",
      "RH-007"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-007"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "DIRECTOR"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the director's office, the specific cadence of his voice when he is testing her",
    "emotional_signature": "the double recognition: he is probing, and she knows he is probing, and he knows she knows",
    "body": "The director is briefing her on a situation that affects the protectee's school. His cadence is wrong. He is giving her more detail than necessary. He is pausing at specific points. He is watching her response. She recognizes the pattern because she uses it. He is probing. He wants to see if she will escalate her protective response beyond what the situation warrants. He is testing whether her protective love is compromising her operational judgment. She recognizes it because she would do the same thing. She does not react to the probe. She answers the operational question, not the emotional one. She lets him see that she sees. The trust between them is built on this: he probes, she recognizes the probe, she addresses the function, not the behavior. He is checking her instrument panel. She lets him. The instrument panel is working."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-017",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Understanding Pathology Without Excusing It",
    "age": 46,
    "year": "Y46",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-017",
      "FP-012",
      "FP-018"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-004",
