    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "a briefing room, the moment when an asset's stated intention diverges from their behavioral trajectory",
    "emotional_signature": "the confidence of someone who knows that what people do matters more than what they say",
    "body": "The asset says he will deliver the information by Friday. His body language says otherwise. The stated intention is Friday. The behavioral trajectory is not. She does not record 'asset will deliver by Friday.' She records 'asset intends to deliver by Friday but his attachment pattern suggests he will prioritize his own safety over the timeline, which means Wednesday at the earliest if the threat level drops, or never if it rises.' People do what their psychology drives. The asset is avoidant-attached. He will avoid the risk of delivering information that could be traced back to him. He says Friday because that is what you say to an operative. He will do what his avoidance pattern dictates. She plans around the behavior, not the stated intention. She learned this from the stepfather: what he said and what he did were different things. She stopped believing what people said when she was eight years old. She watches what they do."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-FP-022",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "She Knows How He Thinks Because She Was Almost Him",
    "age": 35,
    "year": "Y35",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "FP-011",
      "FP-012",
      "SG-004",
      "FP-019"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-FP-003",
      "SD-FP-019"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the criminal's logic — she recognizes it because she grew up inside a version of it",
    "emotional_signature": "the dangerous knowledge: she understands criminal thinking not because she studied it, but because she survived it",
    "body": "The target thinks he is smarter than the system. He believes the rules are for other people. He justifies every violation with a narrative in which he is the victim and the system is the oppressor. She recognizes the pattern. Not from training. From the kitchen table. The stepfather thought the same way. He believed his violence was justified by her mother's failures. He believed the rules were for other people. He believed he was the victim. She grew up inside that logic. She knows how it works from the inside. She knows the rationalizations because she heard them every day. She knows the escalation because she watched it. She knows the self-justification because it was the air she breathed. This is dangerous knowledge. She uses it to read targets. She does not use it to become them. The firewall is: she knows the pattern, and she knows it is a pattern, and she knows what it cost her. The understanding is not romanticized. It is not clinical. It is experienced, then studied. She is the counter-example: someone who grew up inside criminal thinking and chose differently."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-G-001",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Angle of the Door Determines Survival",
    "age": 13,
    "year": "Y13",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "G-004",
      "G-002",
      "S-003",
      "FP-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-S-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-G-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "her bedroom door — the angle it opens determines whether she can see the hallway",
    "emotional_signature": "geometry as survival: the angle is the variable",
    "body": "Her bedroom door opens inward. The hinge is on the left. When it opens, it reveals the hallway to the right. She cannot see the left side of the hallway from her bed. She positions her pillow so she can see the door's angle without sitting up. A door that opens 30 degrees means someone is looking in. A door that opens 90 degrees means someone is coming in. A door that opens slowly means he is measuring her response. A door that opens fast means he has already decided. The angle of the door is not decoration. It is information. She reads angles the way other people read faces. The geometry of the door — its hinge placement, its swing radius, the sight lines it creates or destroys — is load-bearing. A door that opens the wrong way is a design flaw that can kill you."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-G-002",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "Sight Lines and Approach Vectors",
    "age": 22,
    "year": "Y22",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "G-004",
      "G-005",
      "S-001",
      "S-004"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-G-001",
      "SD-S-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the approach to the meeting point — she maps the angles before she walks",
    "emotional_signature": "operational geometry: approach from the blind side, exit at the obtuse angle",
    "body": "The meeting is in a cafe. Corner table. The target faces the door. She approaches from the side entrance, the one he cannot see from his seat. She does not think 'approach vector.' She thinks: the angle. The geometry of approach matters. Coming from the front gives him time to compose. Coming from the side gives her the first read. The stepfather's house taught her this: the angle of approach determines who sees whom first. The grandmother's kitchen taught her the complement: approach from the direction where you are expected, and the encounter is comfortable. She uses both. With targets, she approaches from the blind angle. With allies, she approaches from the expected one. The geometry of approach is not strategy. It is instinct."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-G-003",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "Load-Bearing vs Decorative",
    "age": 31,
    "year": "Y31",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "G-005",
      "G-006",
      "G-001",
      "S-012"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-G-001",
      "SD-S-004"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the organizational chart — she reads it as a structural diagram, not a hierarchy",
    "emotional_signature": "the same question: what holds this up, and what is just surface?",
    "body": "The target's organization has a hierarchy on paper and a structure in reality. The hierarchy is decorative. The structure is load-bearing. The VP of Operations reports to the CEO on paper, but the real authority runs through the logistics manager who controls the supply chain. She reads the structure the way she reads a building: what supports what? Where is the load-bearing wall? Where is the decorative facade? The stepfather's house had a decorative hierarchy (her mother was nominally in charge of the household) and a load-bearing structure (he controlled everything). She learned to read past the facade before she was ten. In organizations, the same skill applies. The org chart is the facade. The actual flow of authority is the structure. Remove the load-bearing element and the structure collapses. Remove the decorative element and nothing changes."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-G-004",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Relationships Have Angles",
    "age": 49,
    "year": "Y49",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "G-007",
