      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee and his friend — the angle between them tells her the state of the friendship",
    "emotional_signature": "reading social geometry the same way she reads physical geometry",
    "body": "The protectee and his friend are sitting on the couch. The angle between them is open — they face each other, shoulders aligned, lean forward. This is a stable geometry. A week ago, the angle was closed — they sat parallel, not facing, a gap between them. She reads the angle the same way she reads a room. Relationships have geometry. The angle between two people — how they sit, how they stand, how they walk together — tells her more than their words do. The stepfather and her mother sat at right angles: she facing him, he facing the television. The grandmother and the child sat facing each other across the table. The geometry of a relationship is not metaphorical. It is physical. It is information."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-G-005",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "Too Much on One Side",
    "age": 40,
    "year": "Y40",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "G-003",
      "EC-005",
      "D-007"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-G-003",
      "SD-EC-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the schedule — too many activities on one day, the system tilts",
    "emotional_signature": "proportion: too much weight on one side and the whole thing falls over",
    "body": "His schedule is unbalanced. Three activities on Tuesday. Nothing on Wednesday. The system tilts. He is exhausted on Tuesday night. He is restless on Wednesday afternoon. She redistributes. Two activities on Tuesday, one on Wednesday. The system stabilizes. Proportion. Too much weight on one side and the whole structure falls over. The grandmother knew this: you do not put all the pots on the front burner. You distribute the weight. The stepfather did not know this. Everything was about him. Every dinner, every evening, every weekend. The house tilted toward him. Everything fell toward the center of gravity he created. She redistributes. She balances the load. Not because balance is virtuous. Because imbalance is unstable. An unstable system breaks under pressure. A balanced system bends. She prefers bending."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-MC-004",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Fixing the Protectee's Problem",
    "age": 53,
    "year": "Y53",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "MC-005",
      "MC-007",
      "B-007",
      "R-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-MC-001",
      "SD-MC-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee's broken project — she diagnoses, she does not fix it for him",
    "emotional_signature": "the hardest craft: diagnosing without fixing, letting him repair it himself",
    "body": "His project is broken. Not literally. The structure is wrong. He is frustrated. He wants her to fix it. She does not fix it. She asks: where does it break? He points. She asks: what holds that part up? He looks. He finds the weak connection. She asks: how would you make it stronger? He thinks. He has a solution. She does not tell him if it is right. She lets him try. The grandmother never fixed the child's problems. She helped the child diagnose them. Diagnosis is a craft. Fixing is a craft. The hardest craft is diagnosing without fixing — helping someone find the cause without solving the problem for them. The cause is his to find. The fix is his to make. She provides the method, not the solution. The grandmother provided the method. The grandmother never provided the solution."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-NI-004",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Protectee Tells Stories Too",
    "age": 52,
    "year": "Y52",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "NI-007",
      "NI-008",
      "R-003",
      "RR-008"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-NI-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee telling a story about school — she listens for the arc, not the events",
    "emotional_signature": "hearing what the story means, not just what happened",
    "body": "He tells a story about school. A kid said something. Another kid did something. The teacher responded. She listens for the arc. Where does he place himself in the story? Is he the hero, the victim, the observer? Where does the story turn? Does it resolve or trail off? The narrative structure tells her his state. A story where he is the observer: he is disengaged. A story where he is the hero: he is confident. A story where he is the victim: he is processing something. She does not interrupt. She does not correct. She lets the story complete. Then she asks the question that the grandmother never had to ask because the grandmother could read the story in the child's face: 'And then what happened?' The question is not about the plot. The question is about the arc."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RH-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Stepfather's Silence Is an Enthymeme",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RH-004",
      "RH-001",
      "RH-003",
      "RR-010"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-RH-003",
      "SD-FP-001"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the silence after dinner — he does not say what he wants, but the warrant fills the room",
    "emotional_signature": "the unstated argument that works because the audience fills in the gap",
    "body": "He does not say what he wants. He does not need to. The silence after dinner is an enthymeme. The claim is implicit: something is wrong. The evidence is the silence itself. The warrant is unstated: you already know what you did wrong. The argument works precisely because he does not make it. If he said it, she could disagree. If he stated the claim, she could challenge the evidence. But the silence cannot be argued with because the silence is not an argument. It is an argument-shaped absence. She fills in the gap herself. She tries to remember what she might have done. She tries to anticipate what will fix it. The enthymeme is the most powerful rhetorical form because the audience co-constructs it. She will use this form later, deliberately, in operations. She learned it from the worst teacher."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RH-006",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "The Protectee's Argument Is Also an Argument",
    "age": 46,
    "year": "Y46",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RH-001",
      "RH-002",
      "RH-011",
      "R-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RH-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the protectee making a case for a later bedtime — claim, evidence, warrant, all there",
    "emotional_signature": "the pleasure of watching a child learn to argue",
    "body": "He wants a later bedtime. He has prepared a case. The claim: I am old enough. The evidence: I finish my homework, I wake up on time, I am not tired in the mornings. The warrant — unstated but present: kids who do these things are old enough. She listens. She does not interrupt. She identifies the stasis point: the disagreement is about what 'old enough' means, not about the evidence. The evidence is good. The warrant is where they disagree. She could grant the claim on a trial basis. She could challenge the warrant by asking what happens when he is tired. She does both. He argues back. She is pleased. Not because he is winning. Because he is learning that arguments have structure, that claims need evidence, that warrants can be questioned. The grandmother never taught her to argue. The stepfather never allowed it. She is teaching the protectee something she never learned in childhood: that disagreement can be structural, not emotional."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RH-008",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "Recognizing Rhetorical Manipulation Aimed at Her",
    "age": 37,
    "year": "Y37",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RH-007",
      "FP-016",
      "FP-008"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RH-001",
      "SD-FP-008"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sales pitch from the contractor — charm, urgency, the unstated warrant",
    "emotional_signature": "cold recognition: someone is constructing an argument to move me",
    "body": "The contractor is charming. He is urgent. He is offering a discount that expires today. The claim is: you need this work done. The evidence is his inspection report. The warrant — unstated — is: if you do not do this, something bad will happen. She reads the rhetorical structure of the pitch. The urgency is artificial — a kairos move, creating a false deadline. The charm is evidence designed to lower resistance. The unstated warrant is fear. She has seen this before. The stepfather's arguments had the same structure: claim (you did something wrong), evidence (the silence), warrant (something bad will happen if you do not fix it). She does not buy. She does not get angry. She identifies the manipulation and declines politely. The skill is not in resisting the argument. The skill is in seeing the argument before it lands."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RH-009",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Persuasion vs Compulsion",
    "age": 50,
    "year": "Y50",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RH-012",
      "FP-014",
      "SG-003"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RH-003",
      "SD-RH-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the choice between two approaches — one persuades, one compels",
    "emotional_signature": "the discipline: persuasion changes minds, compulsion changes behavior",
    "body": "The asset is reluctant. She could compel. She has leverage. She could make him cooperate by threatening exposure. Compulsion changes behavior. But compelled behavior is fragile — it lasts only as long as the pressure does. She could persuade. She could make him see that cooperation is in his interest. Persuasion changes minds. Changed minds cooperate without pressure. She chooses persuasion. Not because it is kinder. Because it is more durable. The stepfather used compulsion. His control lasted only as long as his presence did. The grandmother used persuasion. She never forced a thing, and her influence lasted decades after her death. The distinction is not moral. It is structural. Persuasion is a load-bearing wall. Compulsion is a facade."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RH-010",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "Form Does More Work Than Content",
    "age": 14,
    "year": "Y14",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RH-009",
      "FP-008",
      "SP-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RH-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-RH-004",
      "SD-RH-005"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the stepfather saying something pleasant in a tone that is not pleasant",
    "emotional_signature": "the first lesson in rhetoric: how it is said matters more than what is said",
    "body": "He says something nice. The words are pleasant. The tone is not. The form of the sentence is warmth. The content of the delivery is control. She learns this before she can name it: the form of an argument can do more work than the content. A compliment delivered with a timing that says 'I am watching you' is not a compliment. It is a claim. The claim is: I see you, I can say nice things or not, and you cannot predict which one I will choose. She will use this later, in operations. She will deliver information in a form that does more work than the information itself. She will ask a question that is really a directive. She will make a statement that is really a test. The stepfather taught her the most important principle of rhetoric: the container shapes what it holds."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RR-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "The Stepfather's Footsteps Are a Language",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RR-001",
      "RR-002",
      "SP-001",
      "FP-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-RR-003",
      "SD-FP-003"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of his footsteps on the stairs — heavy, light, deliberate, rushed",
    "emotional_signature": "reading the rhythm of threat before the threat arrives",
    "body": "He is coming up the stairs. She hears the footsteps. Heavy. Slow. Deliberate. This is not the rhythm of a man coming home from work. This is the rhythm of a man who is already at 4 and rising. She has catalogued his footsteps. Heavy-slow: escalation trajectory, currently moderate. Heavy-fast: he is already at 7, something happened. Light-slow: he is at 2, safe for now. Light-fast: he is distracted, heading somewhere with purpose, not about the house. She does not think these categories. She feels them. The rhythm of his footsteps is a language she learned before she learned English. Every rhythm is a different argument. Every rhythm tells her what kind of evening this will be. She arranges herself accordingly."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RR-002",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The House Has a Rhythm",
    "age": 13,
    "year": "Y13",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RR-003",
      "RR-004",
      "RR-002"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RR-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-RR-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER",
      "HER_MOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the rhythm of the house — morning, evening, the shift when his car pulls in",
    "emotional_signature": "the house is a clock and she has learned to read it",
    "body": "The house has a rhythm. Morning: her mother moves quietly, the stepfather is either gone or silent. Afternoon: the house is empty or the grandmother is there. Evening: the rhythm changes when his car pulls in. The shift is audible. The television volume changes. The kitchen sounds change. Her mother's voice changes. The rhythm of the house reorganizes itself around his presence the way water reorganizes around a stone. She reads the rhythm the way a musician reads a score. The tempo is set by whoever has power. The dynamics are controlled by whoever is afraid. The rests — the silences — are the most informative moments, because they tell her who is holding their breath. She has been reading this score since she was four years old."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RR-003",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "Every Target Has a Rhythm",
    "age": 22,
    "year": "Y22",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RR-005",
      "RR-007",
      "FP-005"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RR-001",
      "SD-RR-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the surveillance log — patterns in movement, timing, rhythm",
    "emotional_signature": "the same skill, different target: reading rhythm to predict behavior",
    "body": "The target leaves his apartment at 7:12. He walks to the cafe on the corner. He orders the same thing. He sits in the same seat. He leaves at 7:47. This is his rhythm. A-B-C-pause, A-B-C-pause. She watches for deviations. A deviation in rhythm is information even when the content has not changed. If he leaves at 7:12 but takes a different route, something has changed in his environment. If he leaves at 7:30, something has changed in his intention. She learned this from the stepfather's house: the rhythm of a system is its operating frequency. Deviations from rhythm are the first signal of change. Content changes later. Rhythm changes first. She monitors rhythm before content because rhythm is the leading indicator."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RR-004",
    "bucket": 8,
    "title": "The Silence Taxonomy",
    "age": 28,
    "year": "Y28",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RR-004",
      "RR-006",
      "RR-010",
      "RH-008"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RR-002",
      "SD-RH-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the different qualities of silence — comfortable, guarded, loaded, tactical",
    "emotional_signature": "clinical: silence is not one thing, it is many things",
    "body": "She catalogs silences the way a linguist catalogs phonemes. Comfortable silence: the grandmother's kitchen, no performance required. Guarded silence: an asset who is measuring his words. Loaded silence: the stepfather's silence, the kind that contains a claim. Tactical silence: the deliberate pause she uses to make someone talk. Each silence has a different rhythm, a different texture, a different function. The comfortable silence has no rhythm — it is ambient, like the sound of a kitchen. The guarded silence has a staccato quality — the person is breathing differently, choosing whether to speak. The loaded silence is dense and directional — it points at someone, it demands a response. The tactical silence is light and open — it creates a gap, it invites filling. She learned the first two in childhood. She learned the third in training. The fourth she developed herself."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RR-005",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Reading the Protectee's Rhythm",
    "age": 48,
    "year": "Y48",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RR-008",
      "RR-009",
      "R-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RR-001",
      "SD-RR-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the sound of his backpack hitting the floor — the specific rhythm of his arrival",
    "emotional_signature": "reading his rhythm the same way she read the house's rhythm",
    "body": "He comes home from school. The backpack hits the floor by the door. She hears the rhythm. Fast drop, bounce, slide: good day. Slow drop, thud, no bounce: hard day. No drop, door closes quietly: something happened. She does not ask how school was. She already knows. The rhythm of his arrival is the first data point. She reads it the same way she read the stepfather's footsteps, the same way she read the house's rhythm. The protectee's rhythm is the operating frequency of his day. Deviations from rhythm are the first signal that something has changed. She does not interrogate the deviation. She creates the conditions for him to tell her: dinner is ready, there is no rush, the evening is open. The rhythm of the house is calm. He can enter it at his own pace."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RR-006",
    "bucket": 9,
    "title": "Deviation from Pattern Is Information",
    "age": 41,
    "year": "Y41",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RR-006",
      "RR-007",
      "FP-001",
      "S-009"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RR-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the deviation in a colleague's usual pattern — something has changed",
    "emotional_signature": "the same principle: rhythm deviation is the leading indicator",
    "body": "The colleague always arrives at 8:15. Today he arrives at 7:50. The deviation is 25 minutes. She does not assume he is eager. She reads the deviation as information: something in his environment has changed. A new pressure. A new opportunity. A new threat. She watches his rhythm for the rest of the day. His speech is faster than usual. His eye contact is more frequent. He is compensating for something. She does not need to know what. She needs to know that the rhythm has changed, because rhythm change precedes content change. The colleague will reveal what happened when he is ready. She will already know that something happened because the rhythm told her first."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-RR-007",
    "bucket": 5,
    "title": "The Grandmother's Silence Is Different",
    "age": 16,
    "year": "Y16",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "RR-010",
      "RR-003",
      "FP-018"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-RR-002",
      "SD-FP-005"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-RR-004"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the grandmother's silence — comfortable, ambient, no claim embedded",
    "emotional_signature": "the counter-pattern: silence can be empty of threat",
    "body": "The grandmother is silent. She is sewing. The silence is not loaded. It is not tactical. It is not guarded. It is ambient, the way a kitchen is ambient. The silence does not contain a claim. It does not require a response. It is just silence. The child sits next to her. She does not need to perform. She does not need to monitor. The silence is safe. This is the counter-pattern to every other silence in her life. The stepfather's silence is a weapon. The school's silence is awkward. Her mother's silence is retreat. The grandmother's silence is just quiet. She learns that silence is not one thing. It is many things. The taxonomy of silence is the first taxonomy she masters. It will become the most important skill in her operational career."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-S-001",
    "bucket": 3,
    "title": "Mapping the Kitchen Before She Can Read",
    "age": 10,
    "year": "Y10",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "S-001",
      "S-002",
      "FP-001",
      "SP-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-S-005",
      "SD-S-010"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "GRANDMOTHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the kitchen layout — counter, stove, door, window — mapped before she knew the word 'map'",
    "emotional_signature": "the first map: safety is a geometry problem",
    "body": "She is ten. She walks into the grandmother's kitchen and her eyes move: door, window, counter, stove. She does not think 'exits.' She thinks: there and there. The grandmother is at the stove. The back door is to her left. The window is behind her. The child sits at the table and she is already positioned — she can see the door, the window, and the grandmother without turning her head. She did not decide to sit there. Her body placed her. The spatial mapping is installed before language. She does not know she is doing it. She will always do it. Every room she enters for the rest of her life, her eyes will move first: door, window, position, sight lines. The kitchen is where she learned to map space. It is also where she learned that mapping space is what safe people do. The grandmother never checked the exits. The stepfather's house, she checked them constantly."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-S-002",
    "bucket": 4,
    "title": "The Stepfather's House Is a Geometry Problem",
    "age": 13,
    "year": "Y13",
    "dominance": "identity_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "S-001",
      "S-003",
      "S-005",
      "G-006",
      "FP-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-S-001"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-S-006"
    ],
    "cast_present": [
      "STEPFATHER"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the hallway — long, one exit, his room at the end",
    "emotional_signature": "the geometry of a house that can kill you if you read it wrong",
    "body": "The hallway is long. One end opens to the living room. The other end is his room. Her room is in the middle. The bathroom is past his room. To get to the bathroom at night, she has to pass his door. She maps this geometry before she is old enough to articulate it. The hallway is a single point of failure: one path, one exit. She learns to listen for the sound of his door before she leaves her room. She learns the timing — how long between his door closing and his breathing evening out. The hallway is not a hallway. It is a geometry problem with one solution: wait until the variables are favorable, then move. She will never live in a house with a single exit again. She will never sit with her back to the only door. The geometry of that house installed itself into her body before she had words for it."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-S-003",
    "bucket": 7,
    "title": "Every Room Is the Same Problem",
    "age": 22,
    "year": "Y22",
    "dominance": "operational_dominant",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "S-001",
      "S-004",
      "S-010",
      "G-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-S-001",
      "SD-S-002"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [
      "SD-S-007"
    ],
    "cast_present": [],
    "sensory_anchor": "the brief pause at the door of every room she enters — one second, eyes moving, body orienting",
    "emotional_signature": "automatic: the room is a geometry problem, solved in under a second",
    "body": "She walks into a restaurant. A meeting room. A hotel lobby. A school hallway. Every room is the same problem. Where are the exits? Where are the sight lines? Where is the bottleneck? Where is the dead zone? She solves it in under a second. Her eyes move: door, window, corner, position. Her body orients: maximum options, minimum exposure. She does not think about this. She does not decide to do it. It fires before conscious thought, the same way her hand fires toward a hot stove before she feels the heat. The grandmother's kitchen taught her the geometry of safe spaces. The stepfather's house taught her the geometry of unsafe ones. Together, they installed a spatial processor that runs in the background of every room she enters for the rest of her life."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-S-005",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "She Positions Herself Like the Grandmother Positioned the Cutting Board",
    "age": 45,
    "year": "Y45",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": true,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
      "S-004",
      "S-019",
      "S-010",
      "D-001"
    ],
    "echoes_from": [
      "SD-S-001",
      "SD-S-003"
    ],
    "echoes_to": [],
    "cast_present": [
      "PROTECTEE"
    ],
    "sensory_anchor": "the school auditorium — she sits where she can see the entrance and the stage where he is",
    "emotional_signature": "the geometry of protection: maximum options, minimum exposure, same as the kitchen",
    "body": "The school auditorium. The protectee is on stage. She sits in the third row, aisle seat. She can see the entrance. She can see the stage. She can see most of the audience. The exit is four seats to her left. She did not think about this. Her body placed her, the same way it placed her at the grandmother's kitchen table. The geometry of protection is the same as the geometry of cooking: mise en place, everything within reach, everything visible, no blind spots. The grandmother did not plan where to put the cutting board. She put it where her hand would reach without looking. The granddaughter does not plan where to sit. She sits where she can see without turning. The spatial reasoning runs in both modes simultaneously. In the kitchen, it runs as domestic comfort. In the auditorium, it runs as protective surveillance. The geometry is the same."
  },
  {
    "id": "SD-S-008",
    "bucket": 10,
    "title": "Time Is Spatial",
    "age": 48,
    "year": "Y48",
    "dominance": "integrated",
    "load_bearing": false,
    "behaviors_encoded": [
